= Deep Play: Notes on the Balinese Cockfight / 深层游戏：巴厘岛斗鸡札记

#html.p(id: "en-S001")[#text("Early in April of 1958, my wife and I arrived, malarial and diffident, in a Balinese village we intended, as anthropologists, to study. A small place, about five hundred people, and relatively remote, it was its own world. We were intruders, professional ones, and the villagers dealt with us as Balinese seem always to deal with people not part of their life who yet press themselves upon them: as though we were not there. For them, and to a degree for ourselves, we were nonpersons, specters, invisible men.")]

#zh(id: "S001")[#text("1958 年 4 月初，我和妻子带着疟疾和羞怯来到巴厘岛的一个村庄，我们打算作为人类学家进行研究。地方不大，五百人左右，又比较偏僻，却是一片自己的天地。我们是入侵者，是专业人士，村民们对待我们就像巴厘人对待我们一样，似乎总是对待那些不属于他们生活的人，但他们却向他们施加压力：就好像我们不在那里一样。对于他们来说，在某种程度上对于我们自己来说，我们都是非人、幽灵、隐形人。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S002")[#text("We moved into an extended family compound (that had been arranged before through the provincial government) belonging to one of the four major factions in village life. But except for our landlord and the village chief, whose cousin and brother-in-law he was, everyone ignored us in a way only a Balinese can do. As we wandered around, uncertain, wistful, eager to please, people seemed to look right through us with a gaze focused several yards behind us on some more actual stone or tree. Almost nobody greeted us; but nobody scowled or said anything unpleasant to us either, which would have been almost as satisfactory. If we ventured to approach someone (something one is powerfully inhibited from doing in such an atmosphere), he moved, negligently but definitively, away. If, seated or leaning against a wall, we had him trapped, he said nothing at all, or mumbled what for the Balinese is the ultimate nonword– “yes.” The indifference, of course, was studied; the villagers were watching every move we made and they had an enormous amount of quite accurate information about who we were and what we were going to be doing. But they acted as if we simply did not exist, which, in fact, as this behavior was designed to inform us, we did not, or anyway not yet.")]

#zh(id: "S002")[#text("我们搬进了一个大家庭大院（之前是通过省政府安排的），属于乡村生活的四大派别之一。但除了我们的房东和村长（他是他的表弟和妹夫）之外，每个人都忽视我们，只有巴厘岛人才能做到这一点。当我们四处闲逛时，不确定、渴望、渴望取悦，人们似乎在看穿我们，目光聚焦在我们身后几码远的一些更真实的石头或树上。几乎没有人向我们打招呼；但也没有人皱眉或说任何令我们不愉快的话，这几乎同样令人满意。如果我们冒险接近某人（在这种气氛下，人们会强烈禁止这样做），他就会疏忽但明确地走开。如果他坐着或靠在墙上，我们把他困住了，他什么也不说，或者咕哝着，对巴厘岛人来说，这是终极的非字眼——“是的”。当然，这种冷漠是经过研究的。村民们注视着我们的一举一动，他们掌握了大量关于我们是谁以及我们要做什么的非常准确的信息。但他们的表现就好像我们根本不存在一样，事实上，这种行为的目的是告诉我们，我们不存在，或者无论如何还不存在。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S003")[#text("This is, as I say, general in Bali. Everywhere else I have been in Indonesia, and more latterly in Morocco, when I have gone into a new village people have poured out from all sides to take a very close look at me, and, often, an all-tooprobing feel as well. In Balinese villages, at least those away from the tourist circuit, nothing happens at all. People go on pounding, chatting, making offerings, staring into space, carrying baskets about while one drifts around feeling vaguely disembodied. And the same thing is true on the individual level. When you first meet a Balinese, he seems virtually not to relate to you at all: he is, in the term Gregory Bateson and Margaret Mead made famous, “away.”1 Then–in a day, a week, a month (with some people the magic moment never comes)–he decides, for reasons I have never been quite able to fathom, that you are real, and then he becomes a warm, gay, sensitive, sympathetic, though, being Balinese, always precisely controlled person. You have crossed, somehow, some moral or metaphysical shadow line. Though you are not exactly taken as a Balinese (one has to be born to that), you are at least regarded as a human being rather than a cloud or a gust of wind. The whole complexion of your relationship dramatically changes to, in the majority of cases, a gentle, almost affectionate one–a low-keyed, rather playful, rather mannered, rather bemused geniality.")]

#zh(id: "S003")[#text("正如我所说，这是巴厘岛的普遍现象。我去过印度尼西亚的其他地方，最近又去过摩洛哥，当我走进一个新村庄时，人们从四面八方涌来，仔细地打量我，而且常常有一种令人生畏的感觉。在巴厘岛的村庄，至少是那些远离旅游线路的村庄，什么也没有发生。人们继续敲打、聊天、供奉、凝视太空、提着篮子到处走动，而人们却感觉模糊地脱离了实体。在个人层面上也是如此。当你第一次见到巴厘岛人时，他似乎与你根本没有任何关系：用格雷戈里·贝特森和玛格丽特·米德著名的术语来说，他是“离开”。1然后——在一天、一周、一个月（对某些人来说，神奇的时刻永远不会到来）——他决定，出于我一直无法理解的原因，你是真实的，然后他变成了一个热情、快乐、敏感、富有同情心的人，不过，作为巴厘岛人，总是受到严格控制的人。不知何故，你已经跨越了一些道德或形而上学的阴影线。虽然你不完全被视为巴厘岛人（必须是天生的），但你至少被视为一个人，而不是一朵云或一阵风。在大多数情况下，你们关系的整体面貌发生了巨大的变化，变成了一种温柔、近乎深情的关系——一种低调、相当有趣、相当有礼貌、相当困惑的友善。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S004")[#text("My wife and I were still very much in the gust of wind stage, a most frustrating, and even, as you soon begin to doubt whether you are really real after all, unnerving one, when, ten days or so after our arrival, a large cockfight was held in the public square to raise money for a new school.")]

#zh(id: "S004")[#text("我和我的妻子还处于一阵风的阶段，这是一个最令人沮丧的阶段，甚至，当你很快开始怀疑自己是否真的真实时，甚至令人不安，在我们到达十天左右的时候，在公共广场上举行了一场大型斗鸡活动，为一所新学校筹集资金。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S005")[#text("Now, a few special occasions aside, cockfights are illegal in Bali under the Republic (as, for not altogether unrelated reasons, they were under the Dutch), largely as a result of the pretensions to puritanism radical nationalism tends to bring with it. The elite, which is not itself so very puritan, worries about the poor, ignorant peasant gambling all his money away, about what foreigners will think, about the waste of time better devoted to building up the country. It sees cockfighting as “primitive,” “backward,” “unprogressive,” and generally unbecoming an ambitious nation. And, as with those other embarrassments– opium smoking, begging, or uncovered breasts–it seeks, rather unsystematically, to put a stop to it.")]

#zh(id: "S005")[#text("现在，除了一些特殊场合之外，斗鸡在共和国统治下的巴厘岛是非法的（因为并非完全不相关的原因，斗鸡在荷兰统治下），很大程度上是由于激进民族主义往往会带来清教主义的结果。精英阶层本身并不是那么清教徒，他们担心贫穷无知的农民把所有的钱都赌光了，担心外国人会怎么想，担心浪费时间来建设国家。它认为斗鸡是“原始的”、“落后的”、“不进步的”，而且总体上不适合一个雄心勃勃的国家。而且，与其他令人尴尬的事情一样——吸鸦片、乞讨或裸露乳房——它试图以相当不系统的方式制止这种现象。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S006")[#text("Of course, like drinking during Prohibition or, today, smoking marihuana, cockfights, being a part of “The Balinese Way of Life,” nonetheless go on happening, and with extraordinary frequency. And, like Prohibition or marihuana, from time to time the police (who, in 1958 at least, were almost all not Balinese but Javanese) feel called upon to make a raid, confiscate the cocks and spurs, fine a few people, and even now and then expose some of them in the tropical sun for a day as object lessons which never, somehow, get learned, even though occasionally, quite occasionally, the object dies.")]

#zh(id: "S006")[#text("当然，就像禁酒令期间的饮酒或今天吸食大麻一样，斗鸡作为“巴厘岛生活方式”的一部分，仍然继续发生，而且频率极高。而且，就像禁酒令或大麻一样，警察（至少在 1958 年，他们几乎都不是巴厘岛人，而是爪哇人）时不时地感到有必要进行一次突袭，没收雄鸡和马刺，对一些人处以罚款，甚至时不时地将其中一些在热带阳光下曝晒一天，作为实物教训，但无论如何，这些实物永远不会被吸取，尽管偶尔，非常偶然，该物体会死亡。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S007")[#text("As a result, the fights are usually held in a secluded corner of a village in semisecrecy, a fact which tends to slow the action a little–not very much, but the Balinese do not care to have it slowed at all. In this case, however, perhaps because they were raising money for a school that the government was unable to give them, perhaps because raids had been few recently, perhaps, as I gathered from subsequent discussion, there was a notion that the necessary bribes had been paid, they thought they could take a chance on the central square and draw a larger and more enthusiastic crowd without attracting the attention of the law.")]

#zh(id: "S007")[#text("因此，战斗通常在村庄的一个僻静角落半秘密地进行，这一事实往往会稍微放慢行动速度，但巴厘人根本不介意放慢速度。然而，在这种情况下，也许是因为他们正在为一所政府无法提供给他们的学校筹集资金，也许是因为最近袭击很少，也许，正如我从随后的讨论中了解到的那样，有一种观念认为，必要的贿赂已经支付了，他们认为他们可以在中心广场上碰碰运气，吸引更多、更热情的人群，而不引起法律的注意。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S008")[#text("They were wrong. In the midst of the third match, with hundreds of people,")]

#zh(id: "S008")[#text("他们错了。第三场比赛进行到了数百人的时候，")]

#html.p(id: "en-S009")[#text("On the established anthropological principle, When in Rome, my wife and I decided, only slightly less instantaneously than everyone else, that the thing to do was run too. We ran down the main village street, northward, away from where we were living, for we were on that side of the ring. About halfway down another fugitive ducked suddenly into a compound–his own, it turned out–and we, seeing nothing ahead of us but rice fields, open country, and a very high volcano, followed him. As the three of us came tumbling into the courtyard, his wife, who had apparently been through this sort of thing before, whipped out a table, a tablecloth, three chairs, and three cups of tea, and we all, without any explicit communication whatsoever, sat down, commenced to sip tea, and sought to compose ourselves.")]

#zh(id: "S009")[#text("根据既定的人类学原则，在罗马时，我和我的妻子决定，要做的事情也是跑步，只是比其他人稍微慢一点。我们沿着村庄的主要街道向北跑，远离我们住的地方，因为我们在环的那一侧。大约走到一半的时候，另一个逃亡者突然躲进一处院落——原来是他自己的院落——而我们，除了稻田、开阔的田野和一座很高的火山之外，什么也看不见，就跟在他后面。当我们三人跌跌撞撞地走进院子时，他的妻子显然以前经历过这种事情，拿出一张桌子、一块桌布、三把椅子和三杯茶，我们大家在没有任何明确的交流的情况下坐下来，开始喝茶，试图让自己平静下来。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S010")[#text("A few moments later, one of the policemen marched importantly into the yard, looking for the village chief. (The chief had not only been at the fight, he had arranged it. When the truck drove up he ran to the river, stripped off his sarong, and plunged in so he could say, when at length they found him sitting there pouring water over his head, that he had been away bathing when the whole affair had occurred and was ignorant of it. They did not believe him and fined him three hundred rupiah, which the village raised collectively.) Seeing my wife and I, “White Men,” there in the yard, the policeman performed a classic double take. When he found his voice again he asked, approximately, what in the devil did we think we were doing there. Our host of five minutes leaped instantly to our defense, producing an impassioned description of who and what we were, so detailed and so accurate that it was my turn, having barely communicated with a living human being save my landlord and the village chief for more than a week, to be astonished. We had a perfect right to be there, he said, looking the Javanese upstart in the eye. We were American professors; the government had cleared us; we were there to study culture; we were going to write a book to tell Americans about Bali. And we had all been there drinking tea and talking about cultural matters all afternoon and did not know anything about any cockfight. Moreover, we had not seen the village chief all day, he must have gone to town. The policeman retreated in rather total disarray. And, after a decent interval, bewildered but relieved to have survived and stayed out of jail, so did we.")]

#zh(id: "S010")[#text("过了一会儿，一名警察神气十足地走进院子，寻找村长。 （酋长不仅参加了战斗，而且还安排了这场战斗。当卡车开到河边时，他跑到河边，脱掉纱笼，跳进水里，以便他可以说，当最后他们发现他坐在那里，把水倒在头上时，他在整个事件发生时正在外面洗澡，对此一无所知。他们不相信他，并罚款他三百卢比，这是村里集体筹集的。）在那里看到我和我的妻子和我，“白人”，在那里在院子里，警察做了一个经典的双重观察。当他再次找到自己的声音时，他大致问道，我们到底以为我们在那里做什么。五分钟的主持人立刻站出来为我们辩护，对我们是谁、是什么进行了热情洋溢的描述，如此详细、如此准确，轮到我了，我在一个多星期的时间里几乎没有与除了房东和村长之外的活人交流过，我感到惊讶不已。我们完全有权利去那里，他看着这位爪哇新贵的眼睛说道。我们是美国教授；政府已经批准了我们；我们去那里是为了学习文化；我们打算写一本书来向美国人介绍巴厘岛。我们整个下午都在那里喝茶、谈论文化问题，对斗鸡一无所知。而且，我们一整天都没有见到村长，他一定是进城了。警察狼狈地撤退了。经过一段相当长的一段时间后，我们感到困惑，但又庆幸自己幸存下来并没有出狱，我们也是如此。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S011")[#text("The next morning the village was a completely different world for us. Not only were we no longer invisible, we were suddenly the center of all attention, the object of a great outpouring of warmth, interest, and, most especially, amusement. Everyone in the village knew we had fled like everyone else. They asked us about it again and again (I must have told the story, small detail by small detail, fifty times by the end of the day), gently, affectionately, but quite insistently teasing us: “Why didn’t you just stand there and tell the police who you were?” “Why didn’t you just say you were only watching and not betting?” “Were you really afraid of those little guns?” As always, kinesthetically minded and, even when fleeing for their lives (or, as happened eight years later, surrendering them), the world’s most poised people, they gleefully mimicked, also over and over again, our graceless style of running and what they claimed were our panic-stricken facial expressions. But above all, everyone was extremely pleased and even more surprised that we had not simply “pulled out our papers” (they knew about those too) and asserted our Distinguished Visitor status, but had instead demonstrated our solidarity with what were now our covillagers. (What we had actually demonstrated was our cowardice, but there is fellowship in that too.) Even the Brahmana priest, an old, grave, halfwayto-Heaven type who because of its associations with the underworld would never be involved, even distantly, in a cockfight, and was difficult to approach even to other Balinese, had us called into his courtyard to ask us about what had happened, chuckling happily at the sheer extraordinariness of it all.")]

#zh(id: "S011")[#text("第二天早上，这个村庄对我们来说是一个完全不同的世界。我们不仅不再是隐形的，而且突然成为所有人关注的中心，成为热情、兴趣、尤其是娱乐的对象。村里的每个人都知道我们和其他人一样逃走了。他们一次又一次地问我们这个问题（我肯定已经把这个故事一个个细节一个个细节地讲了，到一天结束时已经讲了五十遍了），温柔地、亲切地，但相当坚持地取笑我们：“你为什么不站在那里告诉警察你是谁？” “你为什么不直接说你只是看，不是赌？” “你真的害怕那些小枪吗？”一如既往，他们充满动觉，即使在逃命时（或者，八年后，他们投降了），他们是世界上最泰然自若的人，他们兴高采烈地模仿着我们粗鲁的奔跑风格，以及他们声称的我们惊慌失措的面部表情。但最重要的是，每个人都非常高兴，甚至更惊讶的是，我们不是简单地“拿出我们的文件”（他们也知道这些）并声称我们的尊贵访客身份，而是表明了我们与现在我们的村民的团结。 （我们实际上表现出的是我们的怯懦，但也有团契。）即使是婆罗门祭司，一个古老、严肃、半途而废的人，因为与地狱有联系，永远不会参与斗鸡，即使是遥远的，即使是其他巴厘人也很难接近，他也把我们叫到他的院子里，问我们发生了什么事，并为这一切的非凡而高兴地咯咯笑。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S012")[#text("In Bali, to be teased is to be accepted. It was the turning point so far as our relationship to the community was concerned, and we were quite literally “in.” The whole village opened up to us, probably more than it ever would have otherwise (I might actually never have gotten to that priest, and our accidental host became one of my best informants), and certainly very much faster. Getting caught, or almost caught, in a vice raid is perhaps not a very generalizable recipe for achieving that mysterious necessity of anthropological field work, rapport, but for me it worked very well. It led to a sudden and unusually complete acceptance into a society extremely difficult for outsiders to penetrate. It gave me the kind of immediate, inside-view grasp of an aspect of “peasant mentality” that anthropologists not fortunate enough to flee headlong with their subjects from armed authorities normally do not get. And, perhaps most important of all, for the other things might have come in other ways, it put me very quickly onto a combination emotional explosion, status war, and philosophical drama of central significance to the society whose inner nature I desired to understand. By the time I left I had spent about as much time looking into cockfights as into witchcraft, irrigation, caste, or marriage.")]

#zh(id: "S012")[#text("在巴厘岛，被嘲笑就是被接受。就我们与社区的关系而言，这是一个转折点，我们确实“融入”了。整个村庄向我们敞开心扉，可能比以往任何时候都更加开放（我实际上可能永远不会接触到那个牧师，而我们偶然的主人成为我最好的线人之一），而且当然要快得多。对于实现人类学田野工作的神秘必要性——融洽关系来说，在一次恶行袭击中被抓，或者几乎被抓，也许不是一个非常普遍的方法，但对我来说，它非常有效。它导致人们突然异常完全地接受了一个外人极难渗透的社会。它让我对“农民心态”的一个方面有一种直接的、内部的了解，而那些不幸带着他们的研究对象逃离武装当局的人类学家通常无法得到这种了解。也许最重要的是，因为其他事情可能以其他方式出现，它很快让我陷入了情感爆发、地位之争和对我渴望了解其内在本质的社会具有核心意义的哲学戏剧的结合。当我离开时，我花在研究斗鸡上的时间与研究巫术、灌溉、种姓或婚姻的时间差不多。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S013")[#text("Bali, mainly because it is Bali, is a well-studied place. Its mythology, art, ritual, social organization, patterns of child rearing, forms of law, even styles of trance, have all been microscopically examined for traces of that elusive substance Jane Belo called “The Balinese Temper.”2 But, aside from a few passing remarks, the cockfight has barely been noticed, although as a popular obsession of consuming power it is at least as important a revelation of what being a Balinese “is really like” as these more celebrated phenomena.3 As much of America surfaces in a ballpark, on a")]

#zh(id: "S013")[#text("巴厘岛，主要是因为它是巴厘岛，是一个值得研究的地方。它的神话、艺术、仪式、社会组织、养育孩子的模式、法律形式，甚至恍惚的风格，都经过微观检验，寻找简·贝洛称之为“巴厘岛人脾气”的难以捉摸的物质的痕迹。2但是，除了一些附带的评论之外，斗鸡几乎没有被注意到，尽管作为一种大众对消费能力的痴迷，它至少与更著名的巴厘岛人“真实的样子”的揭示一样重要。现象。3 美国的大部分地区都在一个球场上，在一个")]

#html.p(id: "en-S014")[#text("To anyone who has been in Bali any length of time, the deep psychological identification of Balinese men with their cocks is unmistakable. The double entendre here is deliberate. It works in exactly the same way in Balinese as it does in English, even to producing the same tired jokes, strained puns, and uninventive obscenities. Bateson and Mead have even suggested that, in line with the Balinese conception of the body as a set of separately animated parts, cocks are viewed as detachable, self-operating penises, ambulant genitals with a life of their own.4 And while I do not have the kind of unconscious material either to confirm or disconfirm this intriguing notion, the fact that they are masculine symbols par excellence is about as indubitable, and to the Balinese about as evident, as the fact that water runs downhill.")]

#zh(id: "S014")[#text("对于任何在巴厘岛呆过一段时间的人来说，巴厘岛男人对阴茎的深刻心理认同是显而易见的。这里的双关语是故意的。它在巴厘岛语中的运作方式与在英语中的运作方式完全相同，甚至会产生同样的乏味笑话、紧张的双关语和毫无创意的猥亵内容。贝特森和米德甚至提出，根据巴厘岛人将身体视为一组独立活动部分的概念，公鸡被视为可拆卸、自我操作的阴茎、具有自己生命的移动生殖器。 4虽然我没有那种无意识的材料来证实或反驳这个有趣的概念，但它们是卓越的男性象征这一事实是不容置疑的，对巴厘岛人来说正如水往低处流的事实一样显而易见。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S015")[#text("The language of everyday moralism is shot through, on the male side of it, with roosterish imagery. Sabung, the word for cock (and one which appears in inscriptions as early as a.d. 922), is used metaphorically to mean “hero,” “warrior,” “champion,” “man of parts,” “political candidate,” “bachelor,” “dandy,” “ladykiller,” or “tough guy.” A pompous man whose behavior presumes above his station is compared to a tailless cock who struts about as though he had a large, spectacular one. A desperate man who makes a last, irrational effort to extricate himself from an impossible situation is likened to a dying cock who makes one final lunge at his tormentor to drag him along to a common destruction. A stingy man, who promises much, gives little, and begrudges that is compared to a cock which, held by the tail, leaps at another without in fact engaging him. A marriageable young man still shy with the opposite sex or someone in a new job anxious to make a good impression is called “a fighting cock caged for the first time.”5 Court trials, wars, political contests, inheritance disputes, and street arguments are all compared to cockfights.6 Even the very island itself is perceived from its shape as a small, proud cock, poised, neck extended, back taut, tail raised, in eternal challenge to large, feckless, shapeless Java.7")]

#zh(id: "S015")[#text("日常道德主义的语言在男性方面充满了公鸡的意象。 Sabung 是“公鸡”一词（早在公元 922 年就出现在铭文中），被用来比喻“英雄”、“战士”、“冠军”、“男人”、“政治候选人”、“单身汉”、“花花公子”、“淑女杀手”或“硬汉”。一个举止高高在上的自以为是的人就像一只无尾的公鸡，趾高气扬地走来走去，好像他有一只巨大而壮观的公鸡。一个绝望的人做出最后的、非理性的努力来摆脱不可能的处境，就像一只垂死的公鸡向他的折磨者做出最后的冲刺，将他拖向共同的毁灭。一个吝啬的人，许诺多，付出少，而且吝惜，这就像一只公鸡，抓住尾巴，向另一只鸡扑去，实际上却没有与他接触。一个对异性仍然害羞的适婚年轻人，或者一个在新工作中急于给人留下好印象的人被称为“第一次被关在笼子里的斗鸡。”5法庭审判、战争、政治竞争、继承纠纷和街头争论都被比作斗鸡。6甚至连岛屿本身也从它的形状来看就像一只小而骄傲的公鸡，泰然自若，脖子伸直，背部绷紧，尾巴翘起，永远挑战着巨大的、软弱的、不成形的爪哇。7")]

#html.p(id: "en-S016")[#text("But the intimacy of men with their cocks is more than metaphorical. Balinese men, or anyway a large majority of Balinese men, spend an enormous amount of time with their favorites, grooming them, feeding them, discussing them, trying them out against one another, or just gazing at them with a mixture of rapt admiration and dreamy self-absorption. Whenever you see a group of Balinese men squatting idly in the council shed or along the road in their hips down, shoulders forward, knees up fashion, half or more of them will have a rooster in his hands, holding it between his thighs, bouncing it gently up and down to strengthen its legs, ruffling its feathers with abstract sensuality, pushing it out against a neighbor’s rooster to rouse its spirit, withdrawing it toward his loins to calm it again. Now and then, to get a feel for another bird, a man will fiddle this way with someone else’s cock for a while, but usually by moving around to squat in place behind it, rather than just having it passed across to him as though it were merely an animal.")]

#zh(id: "S016")[#text("但男人与鸡巴的亲密关系不仅仅是隐喻性的。巴厘岛男人，或者说大多数巴厘岛男人，会花大量的时间和他们最喜欢的人在一起，给他们梳理毛发，喂养他们，讨论他们，让他们互相较量，或者只是带着一种全神贯注的钦佩和梦幻般的自我陶醉的心情凝视着他们。每当你看到一群巴厘男人蹲在议会棚里或路边，臀部朝下，肩膀向前，膝盖向上，其中一半或更多的人手里会拿着一只公鸡，把它夹在大腿之间，轻轻地上下弹跳以加强它的腿，用抽象的性感来弄乱它的羽毛，将它推到邻居的公鸡上以唤醒它的精神，将它收回到腰部以使其平静。再次。有时，为了感受另一只鸟，男人会用这种方式摆弄别人的阴茎一段时间，但通常是走动蹲在它后面，而不是让它传给他，就好像它只是一只动物一样。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S017")[#text("In the houseyard, the high-walled enclosures where the people live, fighting cocks are kept in wicker cages, moved frequently about so as to maintain the optimum balance of sun and shade. They are fed a special diet, which varies somewhat according to individual theories but which is mostly maize, sifted for impurities with far more care than it is when mere humans are going to eat it and offered to the animal kernel by kernel. Red pepper is stuffed down their beaks and up their anuses to give them spirit. They are bathed in the same ceremonial preparation of tepid water, medicinal herbs, flowers, and onions in which infants are bathed, and for a prize cock just about as often. Their combs are cropped, their plumage dressed, their spurs trimmed, their legs massaged, and they are inspected for flaws with the squinted concentration of a diamond merchant. A man who has a passion for cocks, an enthusiast in the literal sense of the term, can spend most of his life with them, and even those, the overwhelming majority, whose passion though intense has not entirely run away with them, can and do spend what seems not only to an outsider, but also to themselves, an inordinate amount of time with them. “I am cock crazy,” my landlord, a quite ordinary afficianado by Balinese standards, used to moan as he went to move another cage, give another bath, or conduct another feeding. “We’re all cock crazy.”")]

#zh(id: "S017")[#text("在人们居住的院子里，斗鸡被关在柳条笼子里，经常移动，以保持阳光和阴影的最佳平衡。它们被喂食一种特殊的饮食，根据个人的理论有所不同，但主要是玉米，比人类吃玉米时更加仔细地筛选杂质，然后将其逐粒提供给动物。红辣椒被塞进它们的喙和肛门，以给它们带来精神。他们沐浴在与婴儿沐浴相同的仪式准备中，包括温水、药草、鲜花和洋葱，对于获奖公鸡来说，沐浴的频率也差不多。他们的鸡冠被剪掉，他们的羽毛被修剪，他们的马刺被修剪，他们的腿被按摩，他们像钻石商人一样眯着眼睛专注地检查是否有瑕疵。一个对鸡巴充满热情的人，字面意义上的狂热者，可以与它们一起度过一生的大部分时间，甚至那些虽然热情强烈但并未完全消失的绝大多数人，可以而且确实花费了不仅在外人看来，而且在他们自己看来，与它们在一起的过多时间。 “我真是鸡鸡狂人”，我的房东，按照巴厘岛的标准，是个相当普通的爱好者，在搬另一个笼子、再洗一次澡或再一次喂食时常常抱怨道。 “我们都疯了。”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S018")[#text("The madness has some less visible dimensions, however, because although it is true that cocks are symbolic expressions or magnifications of their owner’s self, the narcissistic male ego writ out in Aesopian terms, they are also expressions–and rather more immediate ones–of what the Balinese regard as the direct inversion, aesthetically, morally, and metaphysically, of human status: animality.")]

#zh(id: "S018")[#text("然而，这种疯狂还有一些不那么明显的维度，因为虽然公鸡确实是主人自我的象征性表达或放大，用伊索的术语写出自恋的男性自我，但它们也是巴厘人认为的人类地位的直接倒置（从美学上、道德上和形而上学上来说是动物性）的表达——而且是更直接的表达。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S019")[#text("The Balinese revulsion against any behavior regarded as animal-like can hardly be overstressed. Babies are not allowed to crawl for that reason. Incest, though hardly approved, is a much less horrifying crime than bestiality. (The appropriate punishment for the second is death by drowning, for the first being forced to live like an animal.8) Most de-")]

#zh(id: "S019")[#text("巴厘岛人对任何被视为动物般的行为都感到厌恶，这一点怎么强调都不为过。因此，不允许婴儿爬行。乱伦虽然很难得到认可，但远没有兽交那么可怕。 （对第二个的适当惩罚是溺水而死，对第一个则被迫像动物一样生活。8）大多数 de-")]

#html.p(id: "en-S020")[#text("The connection of cocks and cockfighting with such Powers, with the animalistic demons that threaten constantly to invade the small, cleared-off space in which the Balinese have so carefully built their lives and devour its inhabitants is quite explicit. A cockfight, any cockfight, is in the first instance a blood sacrifice offered, with the appropriate chants and oblations, to the demons in order to pacify their ravenous, cannibal hunger. No temple festival should be conducted until one is made. (If it is omitted someone will inevitably fall into a trance and command with the voice of an angered spirit that the oversight be immediately corrected.) Collective responses to natural evils–illness, crop failure, volcanic eruptions–almost always involve them. And that famous holiday in Bali, The Day of Silence (Njepi), when everyone sits silent and immobile all day long in order to avoid contact with a sudden influx of demons chased momentarily out of hell, is preceded the previous day by large-scale cockfights (in this case legal) in almost every village on the island.")]

#zh(id: "S020")[#text("公鸡和斗鸡与这些力量的联系，与不断威胁要入侵巴厘人小心翼翼地建立自己的生活并吞噬其居民的狭小而干净的空间的兽性恶魔的联系是相当明显的。斗鸡，任何斗鸡，首先都是用适当的圣歌和祭品向恶魔献上血祭，以平息他们食人般的饥饿。未定庙会，不得举办庙会。 （如果忽略了这一点，某人将不可避免地陷入恍惚状态，并用愤怒的灵魂的声音命令立即纠正疏忽。）对自然灾害——疾病、农作物歉收、火山爆发——的集体反应几乎总是涉及他们。巴厘岛著名的节日“沉默日”（Njepi），在这一天，每个人一整天都安静地坐着，一动不动，以避免与突然涌入的、暂时从地狱中被赶出来的恶魔接触。在前一天，岛上几乎每个村庄都会举行大规模斗鸡活动（在这种情况下是合法的）。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S021")[#text("In the cockfight, man and beast, good and evil, ego and id, the creative power of aroused masculinity and the destructive power of loosened animality fuse in a bloody drama of hatred, cruelty, violence, and death. It is little wonder that when, as is the invariable rule, the owner of the winning cock takes the carcass of the loser–often torn limb from limb by its enraged owner–home to eat, he does so with a mixture of social embarrassment, moral satisfaction, aesthetic disgust, and cannibal joy. Or that a man who has lost an important fight is sometimes driven to wreck his family shrines and curse the gods, an act of metaphysical (and social) suicide. Or that in seeking earthly analogues for heaven and hell the Balinese compare the former to the mood of a man whose cock has just won, the latter to that of a man whose cock has just lost.")]

#zh(id: "S021")[#text("在斗鸡中，人与兽、善与恶、自我与本我、被唤醒的男子气概的创造力和放松的兽性的破坏力在一场充满仇恨、残忍、暴力和死亡的血腥戏剧中融合在一起。难怪，按照不变的规则，当获胜公鸡的主人把失败者的尸体——通常被愤怒的主人撕成碎片——带回家吃时，他带着社交尴尬、道德满足、审美厌恶和食人者的喜悦混合在一起。或者，一个在一场重要的战斗中失败的人有时会被迫破坏他的家庭神殿并诅咒众神，这是一种形而上学（和社会）自杀的行为。或者，在寻找天堂和地狱的尘世类比时，巴厘岛人将前者比作一个公鸡刚刚获胜的人的心情，将后者比作一个公鸡刚刚输了的人的心情。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S022")[#text("Cockfights (tetadjen; sabungan) are held in a ring about fifty feet square. Usually they begin toward late afternoon and run three or four hours until sunset. About nine or ten separate matches (sehet) comprise a program. Each match is precisely like the others in general pattern: there is no main match, no connection between individual matches, no variation in their format, and each is arranged on a completely ad hoc basis. After a fight has ended and the emotional debris is cleaned away–the bets paid, the curses cursed, the carcasses possessed–seven, eight, perhaps even a dozen men slip negligently into the ring with a cock and seek to find there a logical opponent for it. This process, which rarely takes less than ten minutes, and often a good deal longer, is conducted in a very subdued, oblique, even dissembling manner. Those not immediately involved give it at best but disguised, sidelong attention; those who, embarrassedly, are, attempt to pretend somehow that the whole thing is not really happening.")]

#zh(id: "S022")[#text("斗鸡（tetadjen；sabungan）在约五十平方英尺的场地内举行。通常他们从下午晚些时候开始，运行三到四个小时直到日落。大约九到十场单独的比赛（sehet）组成一个节目。每场比赛的总体模式与其他比赛完全一样：没有主赛，各个比赛之间没有联系，赛制没有变化，每场比赛都是完全临时安排的。一场战斗结束后，情感碎片被清理干净——赌注已付，诅咒被诅咒，尸体被占有——七、八、甚至十几个人带着一只公鸡漫不经心地溜进拳击场，试图在那里找到一个合乎逻辑的对手。这个过程很少会少于十分钟，而且往往要长得多，而且是以一种非常柔和、拐弯抹角甚至掩饰的方式进行的。那些没有立即参与的人充其量只是伪装的、旁观的关注；那些尴尬的人试图以某种方式假装整件事并没有真正发生。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S023")[#text("A match made, the other hopefuls retire with the same deliberate indifference, and the selected cocks have their spurs (tadji) affixed–razor sharp, pointed steel swords, four or five inches long. This is a delicate job which only a small proportion of men, a half-dozen or so in most villages, know how to do properly. The man who attaches the spurs also provides them, and if the rooster he assists wins its owner awards him the spur-leg of the victim. The spurs are affixed by winding a long length of string around the foot of the spur and the leg of the cock. For reasons I shall come to presently, it is done somewhat differently from case to case, and is an obsessively deliberate affair. The lore about spurs is extensive–they are sharpened only at eclipses and the dark of the moon, should be kept out of the sight of women, and so forth. And they are handled, both in use and out, with the same curious combination of fussiness and sensuality the Balinese direct toward ritual objects generally.")]

#zh(id: "S023")[#text("比赛完成后，其他有希望的鸽子带着同样的故意冷漠退出，而被选中的雄鸽则被贴上马刺（tadji）——锋利的尖头钢剑，四五英寸长。这是一项微妙的工作，只有一小部分人（大多数村庄只有六人左右）知道如何正确完成。装马刺的人也会提供马刺，如果他协助的公鸡获胜，主人就会奖励他受害者的马刺腿。马刺是通过将一根长绳子缠绕在马刺的脚部和公鸡的腿上来固定的。由于我稍后会谈到的原因，每个案例的处理方式都略有不同，并且是一件经过深思熟虑的事情。关于马刺的传说非常广泛——它们只有在日食和月黑时才会变得锋利，应该远离女性的视线，等等。无论是在使用中还是在使用中，它们的处理方式都与巴厘岛人对仪式物品的挑剔和感性的奇怪结合相同。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S024")[#text("The spurs affixed, the two cocks are placed by their handlers (who may or may not be their owners) facing one another in the center of the ring.9 A coconut pierced with a small hole is placed in a pail of water, in which it takes about twenty-one seconds to sink, a period known as a tjeng and marked at beginning and end by the beating of a slit gong. During these twenty-one seconds the handlers (pengangkeb) are not permitted to touch their roosters. If, as sometimes happens, the animals have not fought during this time, they are picked up, fluffed, pulled, prodded, and otherwise insulted, and put back in the center of the ring and the process begins again. Sometimes they refuse to fight at all, or one keeps running away, in which case they are imprisoned together under a wicker cage, which usually gets them engaged.")]

#zh(id: "S024")[#text("固定好马刺后，两只公鸡由它们的饲养员（可能是也可能不是它们的主人）面对面放在环的中央。 9 将一个穿有小孔的椰子放入一桶水中，大约需要 21 秒才能沉入水中，这一时期称为 tjeng，并在开始和结束时通过敲击锣进行标记。在这二十一秒内，饲养员（pengangkeb）不得触摸他们的公鸡。如果（有时会发生）动物在这段时间没有打斗，它们就会被抱起、蓬松、拉扯、刺戳或以其他方式侮辱，然后放回环的中心，然后这个过程再次开始。有时它们根本拒绝战斗，或者其中一个不断逃跑，在这种情况下，它们被一起囚禁在一个柳条笼子里，这通常会让它们交战。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S025")[#text("Most of the time, in any case, the cocks fly almost immediately at one another in a wing-beating, head-thrusting, leg-kicking explosion of animal fury so pure, so absolute, and in its own way so beautiful, as to be almost abstract, a Platonic concept of hate. Within moments one or the other drives home a solid blow with his spur. The handler whose cock has delivered the blow immediately picks it up so that it will not get a return blow, for if he does not the match is likely to end in a mutually mortal tie as the two birds wildly hack each other to pieces. This is particularly true if, as often happens, the spur")]

#zh(id: "S025")[#text("无论如何，大多数时候，公鸡几乎立即互相飞翔，拍打翅膀、用头猛冲、用腿踢腿，动物的愤怒如此纯粹、如此绝对，而且以其自己的方式如此美丽，几乎是抽象的，这是一种柏拉图式的仇恨概念。不一会儿，对方就用马刺狠狠地一击。被公鸡击中的训导员立即将其捡起来，这样它就不会受到反击，因为如果他不这样做，比赛很可能会以致命的平局结束，因为两只鸟会疯狂地将对方撕成碎片。如果像经常发生的那样，刺激")]

#html.p(id: "en-S026")[#text("With the birds again in the hands of their handlers, the coconut is now sunk three times after which the cock which has landed the blow must be set down to show that he is firm, a fact he demonstrates by wandering idly around the rink for a coconut sink. The coconut is then sunk twice more and the fight must recommence.")]

#zh(id: "S026")[#text("当鸽子再次回到它们的饲养员手中时，椰子会沉入三下，之后击中的雄鸡必须放下以表明他是坚定的，他通过在溜冰场闲逛寻找椰子沉降来证明这一事实。然后椰子又沉了两次，战斗必须重新开始。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S027")[#text("During this interval, slightly over two minutes, the handler of the wounded cock has been working frantically over it, like a trainer patching a mauled boxer between rounds, to get it in shape for a last, desperate try for victory. He blows in its mouth, putting the whole chicken head in his own mouth and sucking and blowing, fluffs it, stuffs its wounds with various sorts of medicines, and generally tries anything he can think of to arouse the last ounce of spirit which may be hidden somewhere within it. By the time he is forced to put it back down he is usually drenched in chicken blood, but, as in prizefighting, a good handler is worth his weight in gold. Some of them can virtually make the dead walk, at least long enough for the second and final round.")]

#zh(id: "S027")[#text("在这段大约两分钟多一点的时间里，受伤公鸡的饲养员一直在疯狂地训练它，就像训练师在两轮比赛之间修补受伤的拳击手一样，让它恢复状态，为最后一次绝望的胜利而努力。他往它嘴里吹气，把整个鸡头放进自己嘴里吸吮吹气，把它弄松，用各种药物塞满它的伤口，通常会尝试任何他能想到的办法来唤起它可能隐藏在某处的最后一丝精神。当他被迫把它放回去时，他通常已经浑身是鸡血，但是，就像在职业拳击比赛中一样，一个好的驯手的价值与他的黄金相当。其中一些实际上可以让死人行走，至少足以进行第二轮和最后一轮。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S028")[#text("In the climactic battle (if there is one; sometimes the wounded cock simply expires in the handler’s hands or immediately as it is placed down again), the cock who landed the first blow usually proceeds to finish off his weakened opponent. But this is far from an inevitable outcome, for if a cock can walk he can fight, and if he can fight, he can kill, and what counts is which cock expires first. If the wounded one can get a stab in and stagger on until the other drops, he is the official winner, even if he himself topples over an instant later.")]

#zh(id: "S028")[#text("在激烈的战斗中（如果有的话；有时受伤的雄鸽会在训手手中死去，或者当它再次被放下时立即死去），首先击中的雄鸽通常会继续消灭他虚弱的对手。但这远非必然的结果，因为公鸡能走就可以打架，能打架就可以杀人，关键是哪只公鸡先死。如果受伤的一方能够被刺伤并摇摇晃晃地继续前进，直到另一方倒下，那么他就是正式的胜利者，即使他自己在一瞬间就倒下了。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S029")[#text("Surrounding all this melodrama– which the crowd packed tight around the ring follows in near silence, moving their bodies in kinesthetic sympathy with the movement of the animals, cheering their champions on with wordless hand motions, shiftings of the shoulders, turnings of the head, falling back en masse as the cock with the murderous spurs careens toward one side of the ring (it is said that spectators sometimes lose eyes and fingers from being too attentive), surging forward again as they glance off toward another–is a vast body of extraordinarily elaborate and precisely detailed rules.")]

#zh(id: "S029")[#text("围绕着这一切的情节剧——观众紧紧地围在赛场周围，几乎一片寂静，他们的身体随着动物的运动而移动，用无言的手势、肩膀的移动、头部的转动为他们的冠军欢呼，当公鸡带着凶残的马刺向赛场的一侧倾斜时，他们集体向后倒去(据说观众有时会因为太专心而失去眼睛和手指)，再次向前涌动，他们向另一个人瞥了一眼——这是一大堆非常复杂和精确的规则。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S030")[#text("These rules, together with the developed lore of cocks and cockfighting which accompanies them, are written down in palm-leaf manuscripts (lontar; rontal) passed on from generation to generation as part of the general legal and cultural tradition of the villages. At a fight, the umpire (saja komong; djuru kembar)–the man who manages the coconut–is in charge of their application and his authority is absolute. I have never seen an umpire’s judgment questioned on any subject, even by the more despondent losers, nor have I ever heard, even in private, a charge of unfairness directed against one, or, for that matter, complaints about umpires in general. Only exceptionally well-trusted, solid, and, given the complexity of the code, knowledgeable citizens perform this job, and in fact men will bring their cocks only to fights presided over by such men. It is also the umpire to whom accusations of cheating, which, though rare in the extreme, occasionally arise, are referred; and it is he who in the not infrequent cases where the cocks expire virtually together decides which (if either, for, though the Balinese do not care for such an outcome, there can be ties) went first. Likened to a judge, a king, a priest, and a policeman, he is all of these, and under his assured direction the animal passion of the fight proceeds within the civic certainty of the law. In the dozens of cockfights I saw in Bali, I never once saw an altercation about rules. Indeed, I never saw an open altercation, other than those between cocks, at all.")]

#zh(id: "S030")[#text("这些规则以及与之相伴的有关公鸡和斗鸡的发达知识，都记录在棕榈叶手稿（lontar；rontal）中，作为村庄一般法律和文化传统的一部分代代相传。在比赛中，裁判员（saja komong；djuru kembar）——管理椰子的人——负责其应用，他的权威是绝对的。我从未见过裁判员的判断在任何问题上受到质疑，即使是那些更加沮丧的失败者，也从未听到过，即使是私下里，针对某人的不公平指控，或者就此而言，对一般裁判员的抱怨。只有特别值得信赖、可靠且考虑到规则的复杂性、知识渊博的公民才能完成这项工作，事实上，男人只会带着他们的鸡巴参加由这样的男人主持的战斗。作弊指控也向裁判员提出，尽管这种情况极为罕见，但偶尔也会出现；在不少情况下，雄鸽几乎同时死去，都是由他来决定哪一只先行（如果有的话，尽管巴厘岛人不喜欢这样的结果，但可能会有平局）。与法官、国王、牧师和警察相比，他是所有这些人，在他确信的指导下，战斗的动物激情在法律的公民确定性范围内进行。在我在巴厘岛观看的数十场斗鸡比赛中，我从未见过因规则而发生争执。事实上，除了公鸡之间的争吵之外，我从未见过公开的争吵。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S031")[#text("This crosswise doubleness of an event which, taken as a fact of nature, is rage untrammeled and, taken as a fact of culture, is form perfected, defines the cockfight as a sociological entity. A cockfight is what, searching for a name for something not vertebrate enough to be called a group and not structureless enough to be called a crowd, Erving Goffman has called a “focused gathering”–a set of persons engrossed in a common flow of activity and relating to one another in terms of that flow.10 Such gatherings meet and disperse; the participants in them fluctuate; the activity that focuses them is discreet–a particulate process that reoccurs rather than a continuous one that endures. They take their form from the situation that evokes them, the floor on which they are placed, as Goffman puts it; but it is a form, and an articulate one, nonetheless. For the situation, the floor is itself created, in jury deliberations, surgical operations, block meetings, sit-ins, cockfights, by the cultural preoccupations–here, as we shall see, the celebration of status rivalry– which not only specify the focus but, assembling actors and arranging scenery, bring it actually into being.")]

#zh(id: "S031")[#text("一个事件的横向双重性，被视为自然事实，是不受约束的愤怒，而被视为文化事实，是形式完善的，将斗鸡定义为一个社会学实体。斗鸡就是为某种没有脊椎动物、不足以被称为群体、也没有无结构而被称为群体的东西寻找一个名称，欧文·戈夫曼称之为“集中的聚会”——一群全神贯注于共同的活动流程并在该流程中相互联系的人。10这样的聚会会相遇并散去；它们会聚集在一起，也会散开。参与者有波动；让他们集中注意力的活动是谨慎的——一个重复发生的微粒过程，而不是一个持久的连续过程。正如戈夫曼所说，它们的形式来自于唤起它们的情境，即它们所处的地板；但它仍然是一种形式，而且是一种清晰的形式。对于这种情况，场地本身是在陪审团审议、外科手术、集体会议、静坐、斗鸡等文化关注中创造出来的——正如我们将看到的，这里是对地位竞争的庆祝——它不仅明确了焦点，而且通过聚集演员和布置布景，使焦点真正成为现实。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S032")[#text("In classical times (that is to say, prior to the Dutch invasion of 1908), when there were no bureaucrats around to improve popular morality, the staging of a cockfight was an explicitly societal matter. Bringing a cock to an important fight was, for an adult male, a compulsory duty of citizenship; taxation of fights, which were usually held on market day, was a major source of public revenue; patronage of the art was a stated responsibility of princes; and the cock ring, or wantilan, stood in the center of the village near those other monuments of Balinese civility–the council house, the origin temple, the marketplace, the signal tower, and the banyan tree. Today, a few special occasions aside, the newer rectitude makes so open a statement of the connection between the excitements of collective life and those of blood sport impossible, but, less directly expressed, the connection itself remains intimate and intact. To expose it, however, it is necessary to turn to the aspect of cockfighting around which all the others pivot, and through which they exercise their force, an aspect I have thus far studiously ignored. I mean, of course, the gambling.")]

#zh(id: "S032")[#text("In classical times (that is to say, prior to the Dutch invasion of 1908) when there were no bureaucrats around to improve popular morality, the staging of a cockfight was an explicitly societal matter.对于成年男性来说，带公鸡参加重要的战斗是公民的一项强制性义务。通常在集市日举行的斗殴课税是公共收入的主要来源；赞助艺术是王子们的明确责任； and the cock ring, or wantilan, stood in the center of the village near those other monuments of Balinese civility-the council house, the origin temple, the marketplace, the signal tower, and the banyan tree. Today, a few special occasions aside, the newer rectitude makes so open a statement of the connection between the excitements of collective life and those of blood sport impossible, but, less directly expressed, the connection itself remains intimate and intact. To expose it, however, it is necessary to turn to the aspect of cockfighting around which all the others pivot, and through which they exercise their force, an aspect I have thus far studiously ignored.当然，我指的是赌博。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S033")[#text("The Balinese never do anything in a simple way that they can contrive to do in a complicated one, and to this generalization cockfight wagering is no exception.")]

#zh(id: "S033")[#text("巴厘人从不以简单的方式做任何事情，他们可以设法用复杂的方式来做，对于这一概括，斗鸡投注也不例外。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S034")[#text("In the first place, there are two sorts of bets, or toh.11 There is the single axial bet")]

#zh(id: "S034")[#text("首先，有两种投注，或者 toh.11 有一种单轴投注")]

#html.p(id: "en-S035")[#text("The center bet is the official one, hedged in again with a webwork of rules, and is made between the two cock owners, with the umpire as overseer and public witness.12 This bet, which, as I say, is always relatively and sometimes very large, is never raised simply by the owner in whose name it is made, but by him together with four or five, sometimes seven or eight, allies–kin, village mates, neighbors, close friends. He may, if he is not especially well-to-do, not even be the major contributor, though, if only to show that he is not involved in any chicanery, he must be a significant one.")]

#zh(id: "S035")[#text("中心赌注是正式赌注，由规则网络进行对冲，由两个公鸡主人之间进行，裁判员作为监督者和公众见证人。 12正如我所说，这个赌注总是相对较大，有时很大，从来不是简单地由主人以其名义进行的，而是由他与四五个，有时是七八个，盟友亲戚，村友，邻居，亲密朋友一起提出。如果他不是特别富裕，他甚至可能不是主要贡献者，但是，如果只是为了表明他没有参与任何欺骗行为，他必须是一个重要的贡献者。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S036")[#text("Of the 57 matches for which I have exact and reliable data on the center bet, the range is from 15 ringgits to 500, with a mean at 85 and with the distribution being rather noticeably trimodal: small fights (15 ringgits either side of 35) accounting for about 45 percent of the total number; medium ones (20 ringgits either side of 70) for about 25 percent; and large (75 ringgits either side of 175) for about 20 percent, with a few very small and very large ones out at the extremes. In a society where the normal daily wage of a manual laborer–a brickmaker, an ordinary farmworker, a market porter–was about 3 ringgits a day, and considering the fact that fights were held on the average about every 2.5 days in the immediate area I studied, this is clearly serious gambling, even if the bets are pooled rather than individual efforts.")]

#zh(id: "S036")[#text("在我掌握准确可靠的中注数据的 57 场比赛中，范围从 15 林吉特到 500 林吉特不等，平均为 85 林吉特，分布呈明显的三态：小规模斗殴（35 林吉特两边各 15 林吉特）约占总数的 45%；中型（70 林吉特两边各 20 林吉特）约占 25%；大型（175 林吉特左右各 75 林吉特）的比例约为 20%，极端情况下还有一些非常小和非常大的林吉特。在一个体力劳动者（砖匠、普通农场工人、市场搬运工）的正常日薪约为每天 3 林吉特的社会中，考虑到我所研究的邻近地区平均每 2.5 天就会发生一次打架斗殴事件，这显然是严重的赌博，即使赌注是集体赌注而不是个人努力。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S037")[#text("The side bets are, however, something else altogether. Rather than the solemn, legalistic pactmaking of the center, wagering takes place rather in the fashion in which the stock exchange used to work when it was out on the curb. There is a fixed and known odds paradigm which runs in a continuous series from 10–9 at the short end to 2–1 at the long: 10–9, 9–8, 8–7, 7–6, 6–5, 5–4, 4–3, 3–2, 2–1. The man who wishes to back the underdog cock (leaving aside how favorites, kebut, and underdogs, ngai, are established for the moment) shouts the short-side number indicating the odds he wants to be given. That is, if he shouts gasal, “five,” he wants the underdog at 5–4 (or, for him, 4–5); if he shouts “four,” he wants it at 4–3 (again, he putting up the “three”), if “nine,” at 9–8, and so on. A man backing the favorite, and thus considering giving odds if he can get them short enough, indicates the fact by crying out the color type of that cock–“brown,” “speckled,” or whatever.13")]

#zh(id: "S037")[#text("然而，附带的赌注完全是另一回事。投注不是以中央庄严的、合法的契约方式进行的，而是以证券交易所在场外时的运作方式进行的。有一个固定且已知的赔率范例，从短盘的 10-9 到长盘的 2-1 连续排列：10-9、9-8、8-7、7-6、6-5、5-4、4-3、3-2、2-1。想要支持弱者公鸡的男子（暂时不考虑热门，kebut和弱者，ngai是如何建立的）喊出短边数字，表明他想要得到的赔率。也就是说，如果他喊加萨尔“五”，他希望失败者的比分是 5-4（或者，对他来说，是 4-5）；如果他喊“四”，他希望以 4-3 的比分（再次，他喊出“三”），如果他喊“九”，则以 9-8 的比分，依此类推。一个支持最喜欢的鸽子的男人，如果他能让它们足够短，就会考虑给出赔率，通过喊出那只公鸡的颜色类型——“棕色”、“斑点”或其他什么来表明这一事实。 13")]

#html.p(id: "en-S038")[#text("As odds-takers (backers of the underdog) and odds-givers (backers of the favorite) sweep the crowd with their shouts, they begin to focus in on one another as potential betting pairs, often from far across the ring. The taker tries to shout the giver into longer odds, the giver to shout the taker into shorter ones.14 The taker, who is the wooer in this situation, will signal how large a bet he wishes to make at the odds he is shouting by holding a number of fingers up in front of his face and vigorously waving them. If the giver, the wooed, replies in kind, the bet is made; if he does not, they unlock gazes and the search goes on.")]

#zh(id: "S038")[#text("当赔率接受者（弱者的支持者）和赔率提供者（热门球队的支持者）用他们的喊叫横扫人群时，他们开始关注彼此作为潜在的投注对，通常是从远离赛场的地方。接受者试图喊叫给予者更大的赔率，给予者则喊叫接受者更低的赔率。14在这种情况下，接受者（也就是求爱者）会通过将多根手指举在面前并用力挥舞来表示他希望按照他喊叫的赔率下多大的赌注。如果给予者、被追求者以同样的方式回应，则赌注成立；如果他不这样做，他们就会对视并继续搜索。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S039")[#text("The side betting, which takes place after the center bet has been made and its size announced, consists then in a rising crescendo of shouts as backers of the underdog offer their propositions to anyone who will accept them, while those who are backing the favorite but do not like the price being offered, shout equally frenetically the color of the cock to show they too are desperate to bet but want shorter odds.")]

#zh(id: "S039")[#text("边注是在中心投注完成并宣布其大小之后进行的，包括当失败者的支持者向任何愿意接受他们的人提出他们的建议时，喊叫声不断上升，而那些支持热门但不喜欢所提供的价格的人，同样疯狂地喊出雄鸡的颜色，以表明他们也渴望下注，但想要更小的赔率。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S040")[#text("Almost always odds-calling, which tends to be very consensual in that at any one time almost all callers are calling the same thing, starts off toward the long end of the range (5–4 or 4–3) and then moves, also consensually, toward the short end with greater or lesser speed and to a greater or lesser degree. Men crying “five” and finding themselves answered only with cries of “brown” start crying “six,” either drawing the other callers fairly quickly with them or retiring from the scene as their")]

#zh(id: "S040")[#text("几乎总是赔率跟注，这往往是非常一致的，因为在任何时候几乎所有跟注者都在叫同一件事，从范围的长端（5-4或4-3）开始，然后同样以或多或少的速度和或多或少的程度，一致地向短端移动。男人喊“五”，发现自己只得到“棕色”的呼喊，开始喊“六”，要么很快吸引其他呼叫者也跟着他们，要么退出现场，因为他们的")]

#html.p(id: "en-S041")[#text("As the moment for the release of the cocks by the handlers approaches, the screaming, at least in a match where the center bet is large, reaches almost frenzied proportions as the remaining unfulfilled bettors try desperately to find a last-minute partner at a price they can live with. (Where the center bet is small, the opposite tends to occur: betting dies off, trailing into silence, as odds lengthen and people lose interest.) In a largebet, well-made match–the kind of match the Balinese regard as “real cockfighting”–the mob scene quality, the sense that sheer chaos is about to break loose, with all those waving, shouting, pushing, clambering men is quite strong, an effect which is only heightened by the intense stillness that falls with instant suddenness, rather as if someone had turned off the current, when the slit gong sounds, the cocks are put down, and the battle begins.")]

#zh(id: "S041")[#text("随着训手放出雄鸽的时刻临近，至少在一场中心赌注很大的比赛中，尖叫声几乎达到了疯狂的程度，因为剩下的未完成的投注者拼命地试图以他们可以接受的价格找到最后一刻的合作伙伴。 （如果中心赌注很小，则往往会发生相反的情况：随着赔率增加，人们失去兴趣，赌注逐渐消失，陷入沉默。）在一场大赌注、精心制作的比赛中——巴厘岛人将这种比赛视为“真正的斗鸡”——暴徒场面质量，完全混乱即将爆发的感觉，所有那些挥手、喊叫、推搡、攀爬的人都非常强烈，这种效果只有在强烈的寂静中才会加剧。瞬间突然落下，就像有人关掉了电流一样，当缝锣一响，公鸡就放下了，战斗开始了。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S042")[#text("When it ends, anywhere from fifteen seconds to five minutes later, all bets are immediately paid. There are absolutely no ious, at least to a betting opponent. One may, of course, borrow from a friend before offering or accepting a wager, but to offer or accept it you must have the money already in hand and, if you lose, you must pay it on the spot, before the next match begins. This is an iron rule, and as I have never heard of a disputed umpire’s decision (though doubtless there must sometimes be some), I have also never heard of a welshed bet, perhaps because in a worked-up cockfight crowd the consequences might be, as they are reported to be sometimes for cheaters, drastic and immediate.")]

#zh(id: "S042")[#text("当比赛结束时，从十五秒到五分钟后，所有投注都会立即支付。绝对没有恶意，至少对于下注的对手来说是这样。当然，在提供或接受赌注之前，您可以向朋友借钱，但是要提供或接受赌注，您必须已经有钱在手，如果您输了，您必须在下一场比赛开始之前当场支付。这是一条铁律，而且我从未听说过有争议的裁判员的决定（尽管毫无疑问，有时肯定会有一些），我也从未听说过威尔士赌注，也许是因为在激动人心的斗鸡人群中，后果可能是激烈而直接的，正如据报道有时对作弊者来说。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S043")[#text("It is, in any case, this formal asymmetry between balanced center bets and unbalanced side ones that poses the critical analytical problem for a theory which sees cockfight wagering as the link connecting the fight to the wider world of Balinese culture. It also suggests the way to go about solving it and demonstrating the link.")]

#zh(id: "S043")[#text("无论如何，平衡的中心赌注和不平衡的边赌注之间的这种形式上的不对称，给一种理论提出了关键的分析问题，该理论将斗鸡投注视为连接斗鸡与更广阔的巴厘岛文化世界的纽带。它还建议了解决该问题并演示链接的方法。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S044")[#text("The first point that needs to be made in this connection is that the higher the center bet, the more likely the match will in actual fact be an even one. Simple considerations of rationality suggest that. If you are betting 15 ringgits on a cock, you might be willing to go along with even money even if you feel your animal somewhat the less promising. But if you are betting 500 you are very, very likely to be loathe to do so. Thus, in large-bet fights, which of course involve the better animals, tremendous care is taken to see that the cocks are about as evenly matched as to size, general condition, pugnacity, and so on as is humanly possible. The different ways of adjusting the spurs of the animals are often employed to secure this. If one cock seems stronger, an agreement will be made to position his spur at a slightly less advantageous angle–a kind of handicapping, at which spur affixers are, so it is said, extremely skilled. More care will be taken, too, to employ skillful handlers and to match them exactly as to abilities.")]

#zh(id: "S044")[#text("在这方面需要指出的第一点是，中心赌注越高，比赛实际上越有可能是平局。对理性的简单考虑表明了这一点。如果你在一只公鸡上赌 15 林吉特，即使你觉得你的动物不太有前途，你也可能愿意花甚至钱。但如果您下注 500，您可能非常非常不愿意这样做。因此，在大赌注的比赛中，当然涉及到更好的动物，我们会非常小心地确保雄鸽在体型、一般状况、好斗性等方面尽可能地匹配。通常采用不同的方法来调整动物的马刺来确保这一点。如果一只公鸡看起来更强壮，就会达成协议，将他的马刺放置在一个稍微不太有利的角度——这是一种障碍，据说，马刺固定者非常熟练。我们还将更加谨慎地聘用熟练的管理人员，并让他们与能力完全匹配。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S045")[#text("In short, in a large-bet fight the pressure to make the match a genuinely fiftyfifty proposition is enormous, and is consciously felt as such. For medium fights the pressure is somewhat less, and for small ones less yet, though there is always an effort to make things at least approximately equal, for even at 15 ringgits (5 days’ work) no one wants to make an even-money bet in a clearly unfavorable situation. And, again, what statistics I have tend to bear this out. In my 57 matches, the favorite won 33 times overall, the underdog 24, a 1.4 to 1 ratio. But if one splits the figures at 60 ringgits center bets, the ratios turn out to be 1.1 to 1 (12 favorites, 11 underdogs) for those above this line, and 1.6 to 1 (21 and 13) for those below it. Or, if you take the extremes, for very large fights, those with center bets over 100 ringgits the ratio is 1 to 1 (7 and 7); for very small fights, those under 40 ringgits, it is 1.9 to 1 (19 and 10).16")]

#zh(id: "S045")[#text("简而言之，在一场大额赌注的战斗中，让比赛成为真正的五五十命题的压力是巨大的，并且是有意识地感受到的。对于中型比赛，压力要小一些，对于小型比赛，压力还小一些，尽管总是努力让事情至少大致相等，因为即使是 15 林吉特（5 天的工作），也没有人愿意在明显不利的情况下进行等额投注。再说一次，我所掌握的统计数据也证实了这一点。在我参加的 57 场比赛中，热门球队赢了 33 场，劣势球队赢了 24 场，比例为 1.4 比 1。但如果将数字分成 60 林吉特的中心赌注，则高于此线的比率为 1.1 比 1（12 个热门，11 个弱者），而低于此线的比率为 1.6 比 1（21 和 13）。或者，如果你采取极端的方式，对于非常大的战斗，那些中心赌注超过 100 林吉特的人，比率是 1 比 1（7 和 7）；对于非常小的战斗，即 40 林吉特以下的比赛，比例为 1.9 比 1（19 和 10）。16")]

#html.p(id: "en-S046")[#text("Now, from this proposition–that the higher the center bet the more exactly a fifty-fifty proposition the cockfight is– two things more or less immediately follow: (1) the higher the center bet, the greater is the pull on the side betting toward the short-odds end of the wagering spectrum and vice versa; (2) the higher the center bet, the greater the volume of side betting and vice versa.")]

#zh(id: "S046")[#text("现在，根据这个命题——中心赌注越高，斗鸡就越准确地是五五十命题——或多或少可以立即得出两件事：(1) 中心赌注越高，对投注范围的小赔率端的边注的吸引力就越大，反之亦然; (2) 中心投注额越高，边投注额越大，反之亦然。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S047")[#text("The logic is similar in both cases. The closer the fight is in fact to even money, the less attractive the long end of the odds will appear and, therefore, the shorter it must be if there are to be takers. That this is the case is apparent from mere inspection, from the Balinese’s own analysis of the matter, and from what more systematic observations I was able to collect. Given the difficulty of making precise and complete recordings of side betting, this argument is hard to cast in numerical form, but in all my cases the odds-giver, odds-taker consensual point, a quite pronounced minimax saddle where the bulk (at a guess, two-thirds to three-quarters in most cases) of the bets are actually made, was three or four points further along the scale toward the shorter end")]

#zh(id: "S047")[#text("这两种情况的逻辑是相似的。事实上，比赛越接近平局，长赔率的吸引力就越小，因此，如果要有人接受，赔率就必须越短。仅从检查、巴厘岛人自己对此事的分析以及我能够收集到的更系统的观察来看，情况就是显而易见的。考虑到对边注进行精确和完整记录的难度，这个论点很难以数字形式表达，但在我的所有案例中，赔率提供者、赔率接受者的共识点，一个相当明显的极小极大鞍点，其中大部分（据猜测，大多数情况下是三分之二到四分之三）的投注实际上是在该点上进行的，沿着比例尺向较短的一端进一步移动了三到四个点")]

#html.p(id: "en-S048")[#text("The paradox of fair coin in the middle, biased coin on the outside is thus a merely apparent one. The two betting systems, though formally incongruent, are not really contradictory to one another, but part of a single larger system in which the center bet is, so to speak, the “center of gravity,” drawing, the larger it is the more so, the outside bets toward the short-odds end of the scale. The center bet thus “makes the game,” or perhaps better, defines it, signals what, following a notion of Jeremy Bentham’s, I am going to call its “depth.”")]

#zh(id: "S048")[#text("因此，“中间是公平的硬币，外面是有偏差的硬币”的悖论只是一个表面现象。这两个投注系统虽然形式上不一致，但实际上并不矛盾，而是一个更大系统的一部分，可以说，中心投注是“重心”，越大，外围投注越倾向于小赔率端。因此，中心赌注“创造了游戏”，或者更好地定义了游戏，标志着什么，按照杰里米·边沁的概念，我将称之为“深度”。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S049")[#text("The Balinese attempt to create an interesting, if you will, “deep,” match by making the center bet as large as possible so that the cocks matched will be as equal and as fine as possible, and the outcome, thus, as unpredictable as possible. They do not always succeed. Nearly half the matches are relatively trivial, relatively uninteresting–in my borrowed terminology, “shallow”–affairs. But that fact no more argues against my interpretation than the fact that most painters, poets, and playwrights are mediocre argues against the view that artistic effort is directed toward profundity and, with a certain frequency, approximates it. The image of artistic technique is indeed exact: the center bet is a means, a device, for creating “interesting,” “deep” matches, not the reason, or at least not the main reason, why they are interesting, the source of their fascination, the substance of their depth. The question why such matches are interesting–indeed, for the Balinese, exquisitely absorbing–takes us out of the realm of formal concerns into more broadly sociological and social-psychological ones, and to a less purely economic idea of what “depth” in gaming amounts to.18")]

#zh(id: "S049")[#text("巴厘岛人试图通过使中心赌注尽可能大来创造一场有趣的，如果你愿意的话，“深度”的比赛，这样匹配的雄鸽将尽可能平等和尽可能好，因此结果也尽可能不可预测。他们并不总是成功。近一半的比赛都是相对琐碎、相对无趣的——用我借用的术语来说，“肤浅”的事情。但这个事实并没有更多地反驳我的解释，就像大多数画家、诗人和剧作家都是平庸的事实反驳这样一种观点，即艺术的努力是指向深刻的，并且以一定的频率接近它。艺术技巧的形象确实是准确的：中心赌注是一种手段、一种装置，用于创造“有趣的”、“深刻的”匹配，而不是它们有趣的原因，或者至少不是主要原因，它们的魅力的源泉，它们的深度的实质。为什么这样的比赛很有趣——事实上，对于巴厘岛人来说，非常引人入胜——这个问题让我们脱离了正式关注的领域，进入了更广泛的社会学和社会心理学领域，以及对游戏“深度”意味着什么的不那么纯粹的经济概念。 18")]

#html.p(id: "en-S050")[#text("Bentham’s concept of “deep play” is found in his The Theory of Legislation.19 By it he means play in which the stakes are so high that it is, from his utilitarian standpoint, irrational for men to engage in it at all. If a man whose fortune is a thousand pounds (or ringgits) wages five hundred of it on an even bet, the marginal utility of the pound he stands to win is clearly less than the marginal disutility of the one he stands to lose. In genuine deep play, this is the case for both parties. They are both in over their heads. Having come together in search of pleasure they have entered into a relationship which will bring the participants, considered collectively, net pain rather than net pleasure. Bentham’s conclusion was, therefore, that deep play was immoral from first principles and, a typical step for him, should be prevented legally.")]

#zh(id: "S050")[#text("边沁的“深度游戏”概念可以在他的《立法理论》中找到。19他所说的“深度游戏”是指风险如此之高的游戏，从他的功利主义观点来看，人们参与其中根本不合理。如果一个拥有一千英镑（或林吉特）财富的人在平局中投注五百英镑，那么他赢得的英镑的边际效用显然小于他输掉的英镑的边际负效用。在真正的深度比赛中，双方都是如此。他们都难以应对。为了寻找快乐而走到一起，他们建立了一种关系，这种关系将给参与者带来净痛苦，而不是净快乐。因此，边沁的结论是，深度游戏从首要原则来看是不道德的，对他来说这是一个典型的步骤，应该从法律上加以阻止。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S051")[#text("But more interesting than the ethical problem, at least for our concerns here, is that despite the logical force of Bentham’s analysis men do engage in such play, both passionately and often, and even in the face of law’s revenge. For Bentham and those who think as he does (nowadays mainly lawyers, economists, and a few psychiatrists), the explanation is, as I have said, that such men are irrational–addicts, fetishists, children, fools, savages, who need only to be protected against themselves. But for the Balinese, though naturally they do not formulate it in so many words, the explanation lies in the fact that in such play money is less a measure of utility, had or expected, than it is a symbol of moral import, perceived or imposed.")]

#zh(id: "S051")[#text("但比道德问题更有趣的是，至少就我们在这里的担忧而言，尽管边沁的分析具有逻辑力量，但人们确实热衷于经常参与这种游戏，甚至面对法律的报复。对于边沁和那些与他想法相同的人（现在主要是律师、经济学家和一些精神病学家）来说，解释是，正如我所说的，这些人是非理性的——瘾君子、恋物癖者、儿童、傻瓜、野蛮人，他们只需要受到保护以免受他们自己的伤害。但对于巴厘岛人来说，虽然他们自然不会用那么多的词语来表达它，但解释在于这样一个事实：在这种游戏中，货币与其说是一种已有的或预期的效用的衡量标准，不如说它是一种道德意义的象征，无论是感知的还是强加的。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S052")[#text("It is, in fact, in shallow games, ones in which smaller amounts of money are involved, that increments and decrements of cash are more nearly synonyms for utility and disutility, in the ordinary, unexpanded sense–for pleasure and pain, happiness and unhappiness. In deep ones, where the amounts of money are great, much more is at stake than material gain: namely, esteem, honor, dignity, respect–in a word, though in Bali a profoundly freighted word, status.20 It is at stake symbolical-")]

#zh(id: "S052")[#text("事实上，在涉及较少金额的浅层游戏中，现金的增加和减少更接近于普通的、未扩展的意义上的效用和负效用的同义词——快乐和痛苦、幸福和不幸福。在金钱数额巨大的深层关系中，比物质利益更重要的是：即尊重、荣誉、尊严、尊重——总而言之，尽管在巴厘岛，地位是一个意义深远的词。20它具有象征意义——")]

#html.p(id: "en-S053")[#text("This, I must stress immediately, is not to say that the money does not matter, or that the Balinese is no more concerned about losing 500 ringgits than 15. Such a conclusion would be absurd. It is because money does, in this hardly unmaterialistic society, matter and matter very much that the more of it one risks the more of a lot of other things, such as one’s pride, one’s poise, one’s dispassion, one’s masculinity, one also risks, again only momentarily but again very publicly as well. In deep cockfights an owner and his collaborators, and, as we shall see, to a lesser but still quite real extent also their backers on the outside, put their money where their status is.")]

#zh(id: "S053")[#text("我必须立即强调，这并不是说钱不重要，或者巴厘岛人对失去 500 林吉特并不比失去 15 林吉特更关心。这样的结论是荒谬的。正是因为金钱在这个几乎非物质主义的社会里确实非常重要，所以一个人所冒的风险越多，其他很多东西的风险就越大，比如一个人的骄傲、一个人的镇定、一个人的冷静、一个人的男子气概，一个人也会冒风险，同样只是暂时的，但也是非常公开的。在深入的斗鸡中，斗鸡的主人和他的合作者，以及我们将看到的，在较小但仍然相当真实的程度上，还有他们的外部支持者，将他们的钱花在了他们的地位上。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S054")[#text("It is in large part because the marginal disutility of loss is so great at the higher levels of betting that to engage in such betting is to lay one’s public self, allusively and metaphorically, through the medium of one’s cock, on the line. And though to a Benthamite this might seem merely to increase the irrationality of the enterprise that much further, to the Balinese what it mainly increases is the meaningfulness of it all. And as (to follow Weber rather than Bentham) the imposition of meaning on life is the major end and primary condition of human existence, that access of significance more than compensates for the economic costs involved.21 Actually, given the even-money quality of the larger matches, important changes in material fortune among those who regularly participate in them seem virtually nonexistent, because matters more or less even out over the long run. It is, actually, in the smaller, shallow fights, where one finds the handful of more pure, addict-type gamblers involved–those who are in it mainly for the money–that “real” changes in social position, largely downward, are affected. Men of this sort, plungers, are highly dispraised by “true cockfighters” as fools who do not understand what the sport is all about, vulgarians who simply miss the point of it all. They are, these addicts, regarded as fair game for the genuine enthusiasts, those who do understand, to take a little money away from, something that is easy enough to do by luring them, through the force of their greed, into irrational bets on mismatched cocks. Most of them do indeed manage to ruin themselves in a remarkably short time, but there always seems to be one or two of them around, pawning their land and selling their clothes in order to bet, at any particular time.22")]

#zh(id: "S054")[#text("这在很大程度上是因为在较高水平的投注中，损失的边际负效用是如此之大，以至于从事这种投注就是通过暗示和隐喻的方式，通过一个人的鸡巴将一个人的公共自我置于赌注之上。尽管对边沁主义者来说，这似乎只是进一步增加了企业的非理性，但对巴厘岛人来说，它主要增加的是这一切的意义。而且（按照韦伯的说法，而不是边沁的说法），赋予生命意义是人类存在的主要目的和首要条件，获得意义的意义远远超过了所涉及的经济成本的补偿。21实际上，考虑到大型比赛的均等金钱质量，经常参加比赛的人的物质财富的重大变化似乎几乎不存在，因为从长远来看，这些变化或多或少都是平等的。事实上，正是在规模较小、浅薄的斗殴中，人们发现少数更纯粹、上瘾的赌徒参与其中——那些主要是为了钱而参与其中的人——社会地位的“真正”变化（很大程度上是下降的）受到影响。这类人，即柱塞人，被“真正的斗鸡士”高度评价为不了解这项运动的全部内容的傻瓜，以及根本没有抓住要点的庸人。他们，这些瘾君子，被认为是真正的爱好者的公平游戏，那些真正理解的人，可以从中拿走一点钱，通过他们的贪婪的力量，引诱他们对不匹配的公鸡进行非理性的赌注，这是很容易做到的。他们中的大多数人确实在极短的时间内毁掉了自己，但似乎总有那么一两个人在任何特定的时间典当他们的土地并出售他们的衣服以进行赌博。 22")]

#html.p(id: "en-S055")[#text("This graduated correlation of “status gambling” with deeper fights and, inversely, “money gambling” with shallower ones is in fact quite general. Bettors themselves form a sociomoral hierarchy in these terms. As noted earlier, at most cockfights there are, around the very edges of the cockfight area, a large number of mindless, sheer-chance type gambling games (roulette, dice throw, coin-spin, pea-under-the-shell) operated by concessionaires. Only women, children, adolescents, and various other sorts of people who do not (or not yet) fight cocks–the extremely poor, the socially despised, the personally idiosyncratic–play at these games, at, of course, penny ante levels. Cockfighting men would be ashamed to go anywhere near them. Slightly above these people in standing are those who, though they do not themselves fight cocks, bet on the smaller matches around the edges. Next, there are those who fight cocks in small, or occasionally medium matches, but have not the status to join in the large ones, though they may bet from time to time on the side in those. And finally, there are those, the really substantial members of the community, the solid citizenry around whom local life revolves, who fight in the larger fights and bet on them around the side. The focusing element in these focused gatherings, these men generally dominate and define the sport as they dominate and define the society. When a Balinese male talks, in that almost venerative way, about “the true cockfighter,” the bebatoh (“bettor”) or djuru kurung (“cage keeper”), it is this sort of person, not those who bring the mentality of the pea-and-shell game into the quite different, inappropriate context of the cockfight, the driven gambler (potét, a word which has the secondary meaning of thief or reprobate), and the wistful hanger-on, that they mean. For such a man, what is really going on in a match is something rather closer to an affaire d’honneur (though, with the Balinese talent for practical fantasy, the blood that is spilled is only figuratively human) than to the stupid, mechanical crank of a slot machine.")]

#zh(id: "S055")[#text("“地位赌博”与更深层次的争斗以及相反的“金钱赌博”与较浅层次的争斗的这种渐进相关性实际上是相当普遍的。从这些角度来看，博彩者本身就形成了社会道德等级制度。如前所述，在大多数斗鸡活动中，在斗鸡区域的边缘，都有大量由特许经营者经营的无意识的、纯粹碰运气的赌博游戏（轮盘赌、掷骰子、转硬币、壳下豌豆）。只有妇女、儿童、青少年和其他各种不（或尚未）斗鸡的人——赤贫者、社会鄙视者、个人特质——玩这些游戏，当然，赌注水平很低。斗鸡的人会羞于靠近它们。地位稍高于这些人的是那些虽然自己不斗鸡但在边缘较小的比赛上下注的人。其次，还有一些人在小型比赛中斗鸡，或者偶尔在中型比赛中斗鸡，但没有资格参加大型比赛，尽管他们可能偶尔会在这些比赛中下注。最后，还有一些人，社区中真正重要的成员，当地生活围绕着他们的坚实公民，他们参加更大的战斗，并在他们周围下注。这些男性是这些集中聚会中的焦点元素，他们通常主导和定义这项运动，就像他们主导和定义社会一样。当巴厘岛男性以一种近乎崇敬的方式谈论“真正的斗鸡”、bebatoh（“赌徒”）或 djuru kurung（“笼子饲养员”）时，他就是这类人，而不是那些将豌豆和贝壳游戏的心态带入完全不同的、不恰当的斗鸡背景中的人，即被驱使的赌徒（potét，这个词具有小偷或小偷的次要含义）。被遗弃者），他们指的是渴望的食客。对于这样一个人来说，比赛中真正发生的事情更接近于一场荣誉事件（尽管巴厘岛人具有实际幻想的天赋，所流的鲜血只是象征性的人类）而不是老虎机上愚蠢的机械曲柄。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S056")[#text("What makes Balinese cockfighting deep is thus not money in itself, but what, the more of it that is involved the more so, money causes to happen: the migration of the Balinese status hierarchy into the body of the cockfight. Psychologically an Aesopian representation of the ideal/demonic, rather narcissistic, male self, sociologically it is an equally Aesopian representation of the complex fields of tension set up by the controlled, muted, ceremonial, but for all that deeply felt, interaction of those selves in the context of everyday life. The cocks may")]

#zh(id: "S056")[#text("因此，巴厘岛斗鸡之所以深入人心，并不是金钱本身，而是金钱导致的结果：巴厘岛的地位等级制度迁移到了斗鸡的主体中。从心理学上来说，这是对理想/恶魔般的、相当自恋的男性自我的伊索式表征；从社会学上来说，它同样是对由受控的、沉默的、仪式性的、但尽管有深刻感受的自我在日常生活背景下的互动所建立的复杂紧张场的伊索式表征。雄鸡可能会")]

#html.p(id: "en-S057")[#text("The easiest way to make this clear, and at least to some degree to demonstrate it, is to invoke the village whose cockfighting activities I observed the closest–the one in which the raid occurred and from which my statistical data are taken.")]

#zh(id: "S057")[#text("要阐明这一点，至少在某种程度上证明这一点，最简单的方法是援引我观察到最接近的斗鸡活动的村庄——袭击发生的那个村庄，我的统计数据就是从那个村庄获取的。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S058")[#text("As all Balinese villages, this one– Tihingan, in the Klungkung region of southeast Bali–is intricately organized, a labyrinth of alliances and oppositions. But, unlike many, two sorts of corporate groups, which are also status groups, particularly stand out, and we may concentrate on them, in a part-for-whole way, without undue distortion.")]

#zh(id: "S058")[#text("与所有巴厘岛村庄一样，这个位于巴厘岛东南部 Klungkung 地区的 Tihingan 村庄组织错综复杂，是一个联盟和反对派的迷宫。但是，与许多企业集团不同的是，有两种同样是地位群体的企业集团特别引人注目，我们可以以部分为整体的方式关注它们，而不会过度扭曲。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S059")[#text("First, the village is dominated by four large, patrilineal, partly endogamous descent groups which are constantly vying with one another and form the major factions in the village. Sometimes they group two and two, or rather the two larger ones versus the two smaller ones plus all the unaffiliated people; sometimes they operate independently. There are also subfactions within them, subfactions within the subfactions, and so on to rather fine levels of distinction. And second, there is the village itself, almost entirely endogamous, which is opposed to all the other villages round about in its cockfight circuit (which, as explained, is the market region), but which also forms alliances with certain of these neighbors against certain others in various supravillage political and social contexts. The exact situation is thus, as everywhere in Bali, quite distinctive; but the general pattern of a tiered hierarchy of status rivalries between highly corporate but various based groupings (and, thus, between the members of them) is entirely general.")]

#zh(id: "S059")[#text("首先，该村由四个大的、父系的、部分同族通婚的后裔群体所主导，这些群体不断相互竞争，形成了村里的主要派系。有时他们两个两个一组，或者更确切地说，两个较大的人与两个较小的人加上所有无关的人；有时他们独立运作。它们内部还存在子派系，子派系内还有子派系，等等，以至相当精细的区分级别。其次，是村庄本身，几乎完全是同族通婚，它与斗鸡场周围的所有其他村庄相对立（正如所解释的，这是市场区域），但它也与这些邻居中的某些邻居结成联盟，在各种超村庄政治和社会背景下对抗某些其他村庄。因此，正如巴厘岛的其他地方一样，确切的情况是非常独特的。但是，高度企业化但基础不同的团体之间（以及这些团体的成员之间）地位竞争的分层等级制度的一般模式是完全普遍的。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S060")[#text("Consider, then, as support of the general thesis that the cockfight, and especially the deep cockfight, is fundamentally a dramatization of status concerns, the following facts, which to avoid extended ethnographic description I will simply pronounce to be facts–though the concrete evidence-examples, statements, and numbers that could be brought to bear in support of them is both extensive and unmistakable:")]

#zh(id: "S060")[#text("那么，考虑一下，作为对斗鸡，尤其是深度斗鸡，从根本上来说是地位问题的戏剧化这一一般论点的支持，以下事实，为了避免扩展的民族志描述，我将简单地宣布它们是事实——尽管可以用来支持它们的具体证据——例子、陈述和数字既广泛又明确无误：")]

#html.p(id: "en-S061")[#text("1. A man virtually never bets against a cock owned by a member of his own kingroup. Usually he will feel obliged to bet for it, the more so the closer the kin tie and the deeper the fight. If he is certain")]

#zh(id: "S061")[#text("1. 一个人实际上从不打赌自己亲属拥有的公鸡。通常，他会觉得有必要为此下注，而且血缘关系越密切，争斗越激烈，他就越是如此。如果他确定的话")]

#html.p(id: "en-S062")[#text("in his mind that it will not win, he may just not bet at all, particularly if it is only a second cousin’s bird or if the fight is a shallow one. But as a rule he will feel he must support it and, in deep games, nearly always does. Thus the great majority of the people calling “five” or “speckled” so demonstratively are expressing their allegiance to their kinsman, not their evaluation of his bird, their understanding of probability theory, or even their hopes of unearned income.")]

#zh(id: "S062")[#text("如果他认为这只鸽子不会赢，他可能根本就不打赌，特别是如果这只鸽子只是二表弟的鸽子，或者比赛很浅的话。但通常来说，他会觉得自己必须支持它，而且在深度比赛中，几乎总是如此。因此，绝大多数人如此明确地称呼“五”或“斑点”是在表达他们对亲戚的忠诚，而不是他们对他的鸟的评价，他们对概率论的理解，甚至他们对不劳而获的收入的希望。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S063")[#text("2. This principle is extended logically. If your kingroup is not involved you will support an allied kingroup against an unallied one in the same way, and so on through the very involved networks of alliances which, as I say, make up this, as any other, Balinese village.")]

#zh(id: "S063")[#text("2. 这一原则得到了逻辑上的扩展。如果你的亲属团体没有参与其中，你将以同样的方式支持一个结盟的亲属团体对抗一个不结盟的亲属团体，等等，通过非常复杂的联盟网络，正如我所说，这些联盟网络构成了这个，就像任何其他巴厘岛村庄一样。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S064")[#text("3. So, too, for the village as a whole. If an outsider cock is fighting any cock from your village you will tend to support the local one. If, what is a rarer circumstance but occurs every now and then, a cock from outside your cockfight circuit is fighting one inside it you will also tend to support the “home bird.”")]

#zh(id: "S064")[#text("3. 对于整个村庄来说也是如此。如果一只外地公鸡与你村里的任何一只公鸡战斗，你会倾向于支持本地公鸡。如果，一种比较罕见但时常发生的情况，一只来自斗鸡场外的公鸡正在与场内的另一只公鸡打斗，你也会倾向于支持“家鸟”。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S065")[#text("4. Cocks which come from any distance are almost always favorites, for the theory is the man would not have dared to bring it if it was not a good cock, the more so the further he has come. His followers are, of course, obliged to support him, and when the more grand-scale legal cockfights are held (on holidays, and so on) the people of the village take what they regard to be the best cocks in the village, regardless of ownership, and go off to support them, although they will almost certainly have to give odds on them and to make large bets to show that they are not a cheapskate village. Actually, such “away games,” though infrequent, tend to mend the ruptures between village members that the constantly occurring “home games,” where")]

#zh(id: "S065")[#text("4.来自任何距离的雄鸡几乎总是最受欢迎的，因为理论上来说，如果不是好雄鸡，男人就不敢带它，而且他来得越远，就越不敢带它来。当然，他的追随者有义务支持他，当举行更大规模的合法斗鸡活动时（节假日等），村里的人会挑选他们认为是村里最好的公鸡，不管所有权如何，然后去支持它们，尽管他们几乎肯定要给它们下注，并下大赌注，以表明他们不是一个小气的村庄。事实上，这种“客场比赛”虽然不常见，但往往比不断发生的“主场比赛”更能弥合村民之间的裂痕。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S066")[#text("village factions are opposed rather than united, exacerbate.")]

#zh(id: "S066")[#text("村里各派非但不团结，相互对立，愈演愈烈。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S067")[#text("5. Almost all matches are sociologically relevant. You seldom get two outsider cocks fighting, or two cocks with no particular group backing, or with group backing which is mutually unrelated in any clear way. When you do get them, the game is very shallow, betting very slow, and the whole thing very dull, with no one save the immediate principals and an addict gambler or two at all interested.")]

#zh(id: "S067")[#text("5. 几乎所有匹配都具有社会学相关性。你很少会看到两只外来的公鸡打架，或者两只没有特定群体支持的公鸡打架，或者得到的群体支持在任何明显的方式上都没有相互关联。当你真正得到它们时，游戏非常浅薄，下注非常缓慢，整个事情非常乏味，除了直接的校长和一两个上瘾的赌徒之外没有人感兴趣。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S068")[#text("6. By the same token, you rarely get two cocks from the same group, even more rarely from the same subfaction, and virtually never from the same sub-subfaction (which would be in most cases one extended family) fighting. Similarly, in outside village fights two members of the village will rarely fight against one another, even though, as bitter rivals, they would do so with enthusiasm on their home grounds.")]

#zh(id: "S068")[#text("6. 出于同样的原因，你很少会看到两只来自同一群体的雄鸽，更罕见的是来自同一亚派系的雄鸽，而且几乎从来不会来自同一亚亚派系（在大多数情况下是一个大家庭）的斗鸡。同样，在村外打架时，两个村里的人很少会互相打斗，尽管作为死对头，他们会在自己的土地上热情地这样做。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S069")[#text("7. On the individual level, people involved in an institutionalized hostility relationship, called puik, in which they do not speak or otherwise have anything to do with each other (the causes of this formal breaking of relations are many: wife-capture, inheritance arguments, political differences) will bet very heavily, sometimes almost maniacally, against one another in what is a frank and direct attack on the very masculinity, the ultimate ground of his status, of the opponent.")]

#zh(id: "S069")[#text("7. 在个人层面上，陷入一种制度化的敌对关系（称为“puik”）的人们，在这种关系中，他们彼此之间不说话或没有任何关系（这种正式关系破裂的原因有很多：夺妻、遗产争论、政治分歧），他们会非常激烈地、有时甚至近乎疯狂地相互下注，坦率而直接地攻击对手的男子气概，即他的地位的最终基础。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S070")[#text("8. The center bet coalition is, in all but the shallowest games, always made up by structural allies–no “outside money” is involved. What is “outside” depends upon the context, of course, but given it, no outside money is mixed in with the main bet; if the principals cannot raise it, it is not made. The center bet, again especially in deeper games, is thus the most direct and open expression of social opposition,")]

#zh(id: "S070")[#text("8. 除了最浅的游戏外，中心投注联盟总是由结构性盟友组成——不涉及“外部资金”。当然，什么是“外部”取决于具体情况，但考虑到这一点，主要赌注中不会混入外部资金；如果校长不能提出，就不会制定。因此，中心赌注，特别是在更深层次的游戏中，是社会反对最直接和公开的表达，")]

#html.p(id: "en-S071")[#text("9. The rule about borrowing money– that you may borrow for a bet but not in one–stems (and the Balinese are quite conscious of this) from similar considerations: you are never at the economic mercy of your enemy that way. Gambling debts, which can get quite large on a rather short-term basis, are always to friends, never to enemies, structurally speaking.")]

#zh(id: "S071")[#text("9. 关于借钱的规则——你可以借钱打赌，但不能借钱——源于类似的考虑（巴厘岛人很清楚这一点）：这样你就永远不会受到敌人的经济摆布。从结构上讲，赌博债务在相当短的时间内可能会变得相当大，这些债务总是属于朋友，而不是敌人。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S072")[#text("10. When two cocks are structurally irrelevant or neutral so far as you are concerned (though, as mentioned, they almost never are to each other) you do not even ask a relative or a friend whom he is betting on, because if you know how he is betting and he knows you know, and you go the other way, it will lead to strain. This rule is explicit and rigid; fairly elaborate, even rather artificial precautions are taken to avoid breaking it. At the very least you must pretend not to notice what he is doing, and he what you are doing.")]

#zh(id: "S072")[#text("10. 当两只公鸡在结构上对你来说是不相关或中立的（尽管，正如前面提到的，它们几乎从不相互相关），你甚至不会问他的亲戚或朋友他在谁身上下注，因为如果你知道他如何下注并且他知道你知道，而你却走另一条路，就会导致紧张。这个规则是明确的、严格的；为了避免破坏它，采取了相当复杂的、甚至是人为的预防措施。至少你必须假装没有注意到他在做什么，以及他在做什么。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S073")[#text("11. There is a special word for betting against the grain, which is also the word for “pardon me” (mpura). It is considered a bad thing to do, though if the center bet is small it is sometimes all right as long as you do not do it too often. But the larger the bet and the more frequently you do it, the more the “pardon me” tack will lead to social disruption.")]

#zh(id: "S073")[#text("11. 有一个特殊的词来表示对赌，这也是“请原谅我”（mpura）的词。这被认为是一件坏事，但如果中心赌注很小，有时只要你不经常这样做就可以。但赌注越大，赌注越频繁，“请原谅我”策略就越会导致社会混乱。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S074")[#text("12. In fact, the institutionalized hostility relation, puik, is often formally initiated (though its causes always lie elsewhere) by such a “pardon me” bet in a deep fight, putting the symbolic fat in the fire. Similarly, the end of such a relationship and resumption of normal social intercourse is often signalized (but, again, not actually brought about) by one or the other of the enemies supporting the other’s bird.")]

#zh(id: "S074")[#text("12. 事实上，制度化的敌对关系（puik）常常是通过在一场激烈的战斗中进行“请原谅我”的赌注而正式引发的（尽管其原因总是在其他地方），将象征性的脂肪投入火中。同样，这种关系的结束和正常社会交往的恢复通常是由敌人中的一个或另一个支持对方的鸟来表示的（但同样，这并不是真正发生的）。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S075")[#text("13. In sticky, cross-loyalty situations, of which in this extraordinarily complex social system there are of course many, where a man is caught between two more or less equally balanced loyalties, he tends to wander off for a cup of coffee or something to avoid having to bet, a form of behavior reminiscent of that of American voters in similar situations.25")]

#zh(id: "S075")[#text("13. 在这种极其复杂的社会体系中，这种情况当然有很多，当一个人陷入两种或多或少平衡的忠诚之间时，他往往会走开去喝杯咖啡或其他东西，以避免必须下注，这种行为让人想起美国选民在类似情况下的行为。 25")]

#html.p(id: "en-S076")[#text("14. The people involved in the center bet are, especially in deep fights, virtually always leading members of their group– kinship, village, or whatever. Further, those who bet on the side (including these people) are, as I have already remarked, the more established members of the village–the solid citizens. Cockfighting is for those who are involved in the everyday politics of prestige as well, not for youth, women, subordinates, and so forth.")]

#zh(id: "S076")[#text("14. 参与中心赌注的人，尤其是在激烈的战斗中，实际上总是领导着他们的团体（亲属、村庄或其他团体）的成员。此外，正如我已经说过的，那些旁观者（包括这些人）是村里更有地位的成员——可靠的公民。斗鸡也适合那些参与日常声望政治的人，而不适合年轻人、妇女、下属等。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S077")[#text("15. So far as money is concerned, the explicitly expressed attitude toward it is that it is a secondary matter. It is not, as I have said, of no importance; Balinese are no happier to lose several weeks’ income than anyone else. But they mainly look on the monetary aspects of the cockfight as self-balancing, a matter of just moving money around, circulating it among a fairly well-defined group of serious cockfighters. The really important wins and losses are seen mostly in other terms, and the general attitude toward wagering is not any hope of cleaning up, of making a killing (addict gamblers again excepted), but that of the horseplayer’s prayer: “Oh, God, please let me break even.” In prestige terms, however, you do not want to break even, but, in a momentary, punctuate sort of way, win utterly. The talk (which goes on all the time) is about fights against such-and-such a cock of So-and-So which")]

#zh(id: "S077")[#text("15. 就金钱而言，明确表达的态度是，它是次要的。正如我所说，这并非不重要；而是重要的。巴厘岛人对于失去几周的收入并不比其他人更高兴。但他们主要认为斗鸡的金钱方面是自我平衡的，只是转移金钱，在一群相当明确的严肃斗鸡者中流通。真正重要的输赢大多是从其他角度来看待的，人们对下注的普遍态度并不是希望能够干净利落、大赚一笔（赌徒上瘾的人除外），而是赌马者的祈祷：“哦，上帝，请让我收支平衡。”然而，就声望而言，你不想收支平衡，而是想以一种短暂的、间断的方式彻底获胜。谈话（一直在进行）是关于与某某公鸡的战斗，")]

#html.p(id: "en-S078")[#text("your cock demolished, not on how much you won, a fact people, even for large bets, rarely remember for any length of time, though they will remember the day they did in Pan Loh’s finest cock for years.")]

#zh(id: "S078")[#text("你的鸡鸡被摧毁了，而不是你赢了多少，这一事实人们，即使是大赌注，也很少会记住很长一段时间，尽管他们会记得他们在潘洛多年来最好的鸡鸡上所做的那一天。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S079")[#text("16. You must bet on cocks of your own group aside from mere loyalty considerations, for if you do not people generally will say, “What! Is he too proud for the likes of us? Does he have to go to Java or Den Pasar [the capital town] to bet, he is such an important man?” Thus there is a general pressure to bet not only to show that you are important locally, but that you are not so important that you look down on everyone else as unfit even to be rivals. Similarly, home team people must bet against outside cocks or the outsiders will accuse it–a serious charge–of just collecting entry fees and not really being interested in cockfighting, as well as again being arrogant and insulting.")]

#zh(id: "S079")[#text("16. 除了纯粹的忠诚度考虑之外，你还必须押注在自己组的雄鸽上，因为如果你不这样做，人们通常会说：“什么！他对我们这样的人来说太骄傲了吗？他必须去爪哇或登巴萨（首都）才能下注吗？他是如此重要的人物？”因此，人们普遍面临着打赌的压力，不仅是为了表明自己在当地很重要，而且还没有重要到看不起其他人，甚至认为自己不适合成为竞争对手。同样，主队的人必须与外面的公鸡打赌，否则外面的人会指责这种行为——这是一种严重的指控，因为他们只是收取入场费，而不是真正对斗鸡感兴趣，并且再次表现出傲慢和侮辱性。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S080")[#text("17. Finally, the Balinese peasants themselves are quite aware of all this and can and, at least to an ethnographer, do state most of it in approximately the same terms as I have. Fighting cocks, almost every Balinese I have ever discussed the subject with has said, is like playing with fire only not getting burned. You activate village and kingroup rivalries and hostilities, but in “play” form, coming dangerously and entrancingly close to the expression of open and direct interpersonal and intergroup aggression (something which, again, almost never happens in the normal course of ordinary life), but not quite, because, after all, it is “only a cockfight.”")]

#zh(id: "S080")[#text("17. 最后，巴厘岛农民自己非常清楚这一切，并且能够并且至少对于民族志学者来说确实能够用与我大致相同的术语来表述其中的大部分内容。几乎所有与我讨论过这个话题的巴厘岛人都说，斗鸡就像玩火，只是不会被烧伤。你激发了村庄和亲属群体的竞争和敌对行动，但以“游戏”的形式，危险而令人着迷地接近公开和直接的人际和群体间攻击的表达（这在日常生活的正常过程中几乎从未发生过），但又不完全是，因为毕竟，这“只是一场斗鸡”。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S081")[#text("More observations of this sort could be advanced, but perhaps the general point is, if not made, at least well-delineated, and the whole argument thus far can be usefully summarized in a formal paradigm:")]

#zh(id: "S081")[#text("可以提出更多此类观察，但也许一般观点即使没有提出，至少也得到了很好的描述，并且迄今为止的整个论点可以用一个正式的范式有效地总结：")]

#html.p(id: "en-S082")[#text("the more a match is . . .")]

#zh(id: "S082")[#text("匹配越多。 。 。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S083")[#text("1. Between near status equals (and/or personal enemies)")]

#zh(id: "S083")[#text("1. 地位接近的平等者（和/或个人敌人）之间")]

#html.p(id: "en-S084")[#text("2. Between high status individuals")]

#zh(id: "S084")[#text("2.地位高的人之间")]

#html.p(id: "en-S085")[#text("the deeper the match.")]

#zh(id: "S085")[#text("比赛越深入。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S086")[#text("the deeper the match . . .")]

#zh(id: "S086")[#text("比赛越深入。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S087")[#text("1. The closer the identification of cock and man (or: more properly, the deeper the match the more the man will advance his best, most closely-identified-with cock).")]

#zh(id: "S087")[#text("1. 雄鸽和男子的认同越接近（或者：更准确地说，匹配越深入，男子就越能提升他最好的、与雄鸽最接近的认同）。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S088")[#text("2. The finer the cocks involved and the more exactly they will be matched.")]

#zh(id: "S088")[#text("2. 参与的雄鸽越优秀，它们的匹配就越精确。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S089")[#text("3. The greater the emotion that will be involved and the more the general absorption in the match.")]

#zh(id: "S089")[#text("3. 涉及的情绪越大，比赛的整体投入程度也越高。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S090")[#text("4. The higher the individual bets center and outside, the shorter the outside bet odds will tend to be, and the more betting there will be overall.")]

#zh(id: "S090")[#text("4. 中心和外围的个别投注越高，外围投注赔率往往越小，整体投注也越多。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S091")[#text("5. The less an “economic” and the more a “status” view of gaming will be involved, and the “solider” the citizens who will be gaming.26")]

#zh(id: "S091")[#text("5. 博彩的“经济”观点越少，“地位”观点越多，参与博彩的公民就越“团结”。 26")]

#html.p(id: "en-S092")[#text("Inverse arguments hold for the shallower the fight, culminating, in a reversed-signs sense, in the coin-spinning and dice-throwing amusements. For deep fights there are no absolute upper limits, though there are of course practical ones, and there are a great many legend-like tales of great Duel-in-the-Sun combats between lords and princes in classical times (for cockfighting has always been as much an elite concern as a popular one), far deeper than anything")]

#zh(id: "S092")[#text("反向论证适用于较浅的战斗，从反向符号的意义上来说，最终以掷硬币和掷骰子的娱乐方式达到高潮。对于深入的战斗来说，没有绝对的上限，尽管当然也有实际的上限，而且古典时期有许多类似传奇般的领主和王子之间伟大的阳光下决斗的故事（因为斗鸡一直是精英阶层关注的事情，同时也是大众关注的事情），比任何事情都深刻得多")]

#html.p(id: "en-S093")[#text("“")]

#zh(id: "S093")[#text("“")]

#html.p(id: "en-S094")[#text("Indeed, one of the great culture heroes of Bali is a prince, called after his passion for the sport, “The Cockfighter,” who happened to be away at a very deep cockfight with a neighboring prince when the whole of his family–father, brothers, wives, sisters–were assassinated by commoner usurpers. Thus spared, he returned to dispatch the upstarts, regain the throne, reconstitute the Balinese high tradition, and build its most powerful, glorious, and prosperous state. Along with everything else that the Balinese see in fighting cocks–themselves, their social order, abstract hatred, masculinity, demonic power–they also see the archetype of status virtue, the arrogant, resolute, honor-mad player with real fire, the ksatria prince.27")]

#zh(id: "S094")[#text("事实上，巴厘岛最伟大的文化英雄之一是一位王子，因其对这项运动的热情而被称为“斗鸡者”，当他的全家——父亲、兄弟、妻子、姐妹——被平民篡夺者暗杀时，他恰好在与邻近的一位王子进行一场非常激烈的斗鸡。如此幸免于难，他回来消灭暴发户，重新夺回王位，重建巴厘岛的崇高传统，并建立其最强大、光荣和繁荣的国家。除了巴厘岛人在斗鸡身上看到的一切——斗鸡本身、他们的社会秩序、抽象的仇恨、男子气概、恶魔的力量——他们还看到了地位美德的原型，傲慢、坚决、狂热的荣誉玩家，真正的火力，卡萨特里亚王子。 27")]

#html.p(id: "en-S095")[#text("Poetry makes nothing happen,” Auden says in his elegy of Yeats, “it survives in the valley of its saying . . . a way of happening, a mouth.” The cockfight too, in this colloquial sense, makes nothing happen. Men go on allegorically humiliating one another and being allegorically humiliated by one another, day after day, glorying quietly in the experience if they have triumphed, crushed only slightly more openly by it if they have not. But no one’s status really changes. You cannot ascend the status ladder by winning cockfights; you cannot, as an individual, really ascend it at all. Nor can you descend it that way.28 All you can do is enjoy and savor, or suffer and withstand, the concocted sensation of drastic and momentary movement along an aesthetic semblance of that ladder, a kind of behindthe-mirror status jump which has the look of mobility without its actuality.")]

#zh(id: "S095")[#text("诗歌不会发生任何事情，”奥登在他的叶芝挽歌中说道，“它在它的言说的山谷中幸存下来……。 。 。一种发生的方式，一张嘴。”从这个口语的意义上来说，斗鸡也没有发生任何事情。人们日复一日地继续寓言式地羞辱彼此，又被寓言式地羞辱，如果他们取得了胜利，他们就会在这种经历中默默地感到自豪，如果他们没有取得胜利，他们只会更加公开地被压垮。但没有人的地位真正改变。你不能通过赢得斗鸡来提升地位；作为一个个体，你根本无法真正提升它。你也不能用这种方式下降。28你所能做的就是享受和品味，或者忍受和承受，沿着梯子的美学外观剧烈而短暂的运动的混合感觉，一种镜子后面的地位跳跃，看起来有流动性，但实际上却没有。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S096")[#text("As any art form–for that, finally, is what we are dealing with–the cockfight renders ordinary, everyday experience comprehensible by presenting it in terms of acts and objects which have had their practical consequences removed and been reduced (or, if you prefer, raised) to the level of sheer appearances, where their meaning can be more powerfully articulated and more exactly perceived. The cockfight is “really real” only to the cocks–it does not kill anyone, castrate anyone, reduce anyone to animal status, alter the hierarchical relations among people, nor refashion the hierarchy; it does not even redistribute income in any significant way. What it does is what, for other peoples with other temperaments and other conventions, Lear and Crime and Punishment do; it catches up these themes–death, masculinity, rage, pride, loss, beneficence, chance–and, ordering them into an encompassing structure, presents them in such a way as to throw into relief a particular view of their essential nature. It puts a construction on them, makes them, to those historically positioned to appreciate the construction, meaningful–visible, tangible, graspable–“real,” in an ideational sense. An image, fiction, a model, a metaphor, the cockfight is a means of expression; its function is neither to assuage social passions nor to heighten them (though, in its play-withfire way, it does a bit of both), but, in a medium of feathers, blood, crowds, and money, to display them.")]

#zh(id: "S096")[#text("与任何艺术形式一样——最后，这就是我们正在处理的——斗鸡通过以行为和物体的形式呈现普通的日常经验，使之变得可以理解，这些行为和物体已经消除了它们的实际后果，并被减少（或者，如果你愿意，提高）到纯粹的表象水平，在那里它们的意义可以被更强有力地表达和更准确地感知。斗鸡只对公鸡来说是“真正真实的”——它不会杀死任何人，阉割任何人，将任何人降低为动物地位，改变人与人之间的等级关系，也不会重塑等级；它不会杀死任何人，也不会阉割任何人。它甚至没有以任何重大方式重新分配收入。它所做的就是《李尔王》和《罪与罚》对于具有不同性情和不同习俗的其他民族所做的事情；它抓住了这些主题——死亡、男子气概、愤怒、骄傲、失落、仁慈、机会——并将它们整理成一个包容性的结构，以一种突显其本质本质的特殊观点的方式呈现它们。它对它们进行了建构，使它们对于那些历史上欣赏这种建构的人来说是有意义的——可见的、有形的、可理解的——在观念意义上是“真实的”。一个形象，一个虚构，一个模型，一个隐喻，斗鸡是一种表达方式；它的功能既不是平息社会激情，也不是煽动社会激情（尽管它以玩火的方式，两者兼而有之），而是以羽毛、鲜血、人群和金钱为媒介，展示它们。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S097")[#text("The question of how it is that we perceive qualities in things–paintings, books, melodies, plays–that we do not feel we can assert literally to be there has come, in recent years, into the very center of aesthetic theory.29 Neither the sentiments of the artist, which remain his, nor those of the audience, which remain theirs, can account for the agitation of one painting or the serenity of another. We attribute grandeur, wit, despair, exuberance to strings of sounds; lightness, energy, violence, fluidity to blocks of stone. Novels are said to have strength, buildings eloquence, plays momentum, ballets repose. In this realm of eccentric predicates, to say that the cockfight, in its perfected cases at least, is “disquietful” does not seem at all unnatural, merely, as I have just denied it practical consequence, somewhat puzzling.")]

#zh(id: "S097")[#text("近年来，我们如何感知事物——绘画、书籍、旋律、戏剧——中我们感觉无法断言其存在的品质，这个问题已经成为美学理论的核心。29艺术家的情感（仍然是他的）和观众的情感（仍然是他们的）都无法解释一幅画的激动或另一幅画的平静。我们把宏伟、机智、绝望、活力归因于声音的串串；石块的轻盈、能量、暴力、流动性。据说小说有力量，建筑有口才，戏剧有气势，芭蕾有休止符。在这个古怪谓词的领域中，说斗鸡，至少在其完美的情况下，是“令人不安的”，似乎一点也不不自然，只是，正如我刚刚否认的那样，它的实际后果有点令人费解。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S098")[#text("The disquietfulness arises, “somehow,” out of a conjunction of three attributes of the fight: its immediate dramatic shape; its metaphoric content; and its social context. A cultural")]

#zh(id: "S098")[#text("“不知何故”，这种不安是由战斗的三个属性结合在一起产生的：它直接的戏剧性的形式；它的隐喻内容；及其社会背景。一个文化")]

#html.p(id: "en-S099")[#text("As a dramatic shape, the fight displays a characteristic that does not seem so remarkable until one realizes that it does not have to be there: a radically atomistical structure.30 Each match is a world unto itself, a particulate burst of form. There is the match making, there is the betting, there is the fight, there is the result–utter triumph and utter defeat– and there is the hurried, embarrassed passing of money. The loser is not consoled. People drift away from him, look through him, leave him to assimilate his momentary descent into nonbeing, reset his face, and return, scarless and intact, to the fray. Nor are winners congratulated, or events rehashed; once a match is ended the crowd’s attention turns totally to the next, with no looking back. A shadow of the experience no doubt remains with the principals, perhaps even with some of the witnesses, of a deep fight, as it remains with us when we leave the theater after seeing a powerful play well-performed; but it quite soon fades to become at most a schematic memory–a diffuse glow or an abstract shudder–and usually not even that. Any expressive form lives only in its own present–the one it itself creates. But, here, that present is severed into a string of flashes, some more bright than others, but all of them disconnected, aesthetic quanta. Whatever the cockfight says, it says in spurts.")]

#zh(id: "S099")[#text("作为一种戏剧性的形式，这场战斗展现了一种看似并不那么引人注目的特征，直到人们意识到它不一定存在：一种根本上的原子结构。30 每场比赛都是一个独立的世界，是形式的微粒爆发。有牵线搭桥，有投注，有战斗，有结果——彻底的胜利和彻底的失败——还有匆忙、尴尬的金钱传递。失败者得不到安慰。人们渐渐远离他，看透他，让他将短暂的堕落同化为虚无，重新调整他的面容，然后毫发无伤地回到战斗中。获奖者也不会得到祝贺，事件也不会被重新讨论；一旦一场比赛结束，观众的注意力就会完全转向下一场比赛，不会回头。毫无疑问，一场深刻的战斗的经历的阴影仍然存在于主要人物身上，甚至可能存在于一些目击者身上，就像当我们在看完一场精彩的戏剧表演后离开剧院时，它仍然存在于我们身上一样。但它很快就会消失，至多成为一种示意性记忆——一种弥漫的光芒或一种抽象的颤抖——而且通常甚至不是这样。任何表达形式都只存在于它自己的当下——它自己创造的当下。但是，在这里，那个当下被分割成一串闪光，有些比其他更亮，但所有这些都是互不相连的，美学量子。无论斗鸡说什么，它都会突然说出来。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S100")[#text("But, as I have argued lengthily elsewhere, the Balinese live in spurts.31 Their life, as they arrange it and perceive it, is less a flow, a directional movement out of the past, through the present, toward the future than an on-off pulsation of meaning and vacuity, an arrhythmic alternation of short periods when “something” (that is, something significant) is happening and equally short ones where “nothing” (that is, nothing much) is–between what they themselves call “full” and “empty” times, or, in another idiom, “junctures” and “holes.” In focusing activity down to a burning-glass dot, the cockfight is merely being Balinese in the same way in which everything from the monadic encounters of everyday life, through the clanging pointillism of gamelan music, to the visiting-day-of-the-gods temple celebrations are. It is not an imitation of the punctuateness of Balinese social life, nor a depiction of it, nor even an expression of it; it is an example of it, carefully prepared.32")]

#zh(id: "S100")[#text("但是，正如我在其他地方长时间论证的那样，巴厘岛人的生活充满了爆发力。 31他们的生活，在他们安排和感知的过程中，与其说是一种流动，一种从过去、穿过现在、走向未来的定向运动，不如说是一种意义和空虚的开关脉动，一种“有事”（即重大事件）发生的短暂时期和“无事”（即没有什么大事）发生的同样短暂的短暂时期的无节律交替。他们自己称之为“满”和“空”时间，或者用另一个习语来说，“接合点”和“洞”。将活动集中到一个燃烧的玻璃点上，斗鸡只是巴厘岛人的方式，就像日常生活中的单子相遇，通过甘美兰音乐的叮叮当当的点彩派，到众神来访的寺庙庆祝活动一样。它不是对巴厘岛社会生活准时性的模仿，也不是对它的描述，甚至不是它的表达；它是一种表达。这是精心准备的一个例子。32")]

#html.p(id: "en-S101")[#text("If one dimension of the cockfight’s structure, its lack of temporal directionality, makes it seem a typical segment of the general social life, however, the other, its flat-out, head-to-head (or spur-tospur) aggressiveness, makes it seem a contradiction, a reversal, even a subversion of it. In the normal course of things, the Balinese are shy to the point of obsessiveness of open conflict. Oblique, cautious, subdued, controlled, masters of indirection and dissimulation–what they call alus, “polished,” “smooth”– they rarely face what they can turn away from, rarely resist what they can evade. But here they portray themselves as wild and murderous, manic explosions of instinctual cruelty. A powerful rendering of life as the Balinese most deeply do not want it (to adapt a phrase Frye has used of Gloucester’s blinding) is set in the context of a sample of it as they do in fact have it.33 And, because the context suggests that the rendering, if less than a straightforward description is nonetheless more than an idle fancy, it is here that the disquietfulness–the disquietfulness of the fight, not (or, anyway, not necessarily) its patrons, who seem in fact rather thoroughly to enjoy it– emerges. The slaughter in the cock ring is not a depiction of how things literally are among men, but, what is almost worse, of how, from a particular angle, they imaginatively are.34")]

#zh(id: "S101")[#text("如果说斗鸡结构的一个方面，即它缺乏时间方向性，使它看起来是一般社会生活的一个典型部分，那么，另一方面，它的直截了当的、头对头（或马刺-马刺）的攻击性，则使它看起来是一种矛盾、一种逆转，甚至是一种颠覆。在正常情况下，巴厘岛人羞于公开冲突。狡猾、谨慎、克制、克制，是间接和掩饰的大师——他们称之为alus、“优雅”、“圆滑”——他们很少面对他们可以逃避的事情，很少抵制他们可以逃避的事情。但在这里，他们把自己描绘成狂野、凶残、本能残忍的狂躁爆发。对巴厘岛人最不想要的生活的有力渲染（改编为弗莱在格洛斯特致盲时使用的短语）是在他们实际上拥有的样本的背景下设定的。33而且，因为上下文表明，这种渲染，如果不是一个简单的描述，但仍然只是一种无聊的幻想，那么这里的不安——战斗的不安，而不是（或者，无论如何，不一定）它的赞助人，他们实际上似乎是这样的。相当彻底地享受它——出现了。公鸡圈中的屠杀并不是对人类之间事物真实情况的描述，但更糟糕的是，从特定角度来看，它们在想象中是如何存在的。 34")]

#html.p(id: "en-S102")[#text("The angle, of course, is stratificatory. What, as we have already seen, the cockfight talks most forcibly about is status relationships, and what it says about them is that they are matters of life and death. That prestige is a pro-")]

#zh(id: "S102")[#text("当然，角度是分层的。正如我们已经看到的，斗鸡最有力地谈论的是地位关系，它所表达的是它们是生死攸关的问题。这种威望是亲")]

#html.p(id: "en-S103")[#text("Any expressive form works (when it works) by disarranging semantic contexts in such a way that properties conventionally ascribed to certain things are unconventionally ascribed to others, which are then seen actually to possess them. To call the wind a cripple, as Stevens does, to fix tone and manipulate timbre, as Schoenberg does, or, closer to our case, to picture an art critic as a dissolute bear, as Hogarth does, is to cross conceptual wires; the established conjunctions between objects and their qualities are altered and phenomena– fall weather, melodic shape, or cultural journalism–are clothed in signifiers which normally point to other referents.35 Similarly, to connect–and connect, and connect–the collision of roosters with the divisiveness of status is to invite a transfer of perceptions from the former to the latter, a transfer which is at once a description and a judgment. (Logically, the transfer could, of course, as well go the other way; but, like most of the rest of us, the Balinese are a great deal more interested in understanding men than they are in understanding cocks.)")]

#zh(id: "S103")[#text("任何表达形式（当它起作用时）都是通过打乱语义上下文来起作用的，这种方式使得传统上归因于某些事物的属性被非常规地归因于其他事物，然后被视为实际上拥有它们。像史蒂文斯那样称风为跛子，像勋伯格那样固定音调和操纵音色，或者更接近我们的情况，像霍加斯那样将艺术评论家描绘成放荡的熊，都是跨越概念的界限；物体及其品质之间既定的联系发生了改变，现象——秋天的天气、旋律的形状或文化新闻——被披上了通常指向其他指称的能指的外衣。 35同样，将公鸡与地位的分裂性的碰撞连接起来——连接起来，再连接起来，就是邀请从前者到后者的感知转移，这种转移既是一种描述，也是一种判断。 （从逻辑上讲，这种转移当然也可以反其道而行之；但是，像我们大多数人一样，巴厘岛人对了解男人比了解鸡巴更感兴趣。）")]

#html.p(id: "en-S104")[#text("What sets the cockfight apart from the ordinary course of life, lifts it from the realm of everyday practical affairs, and surrounds it with an aura of enlarged importance is not, as functionalist sociology would have it, that it reinforces status discriminations (such reinforcement is hardly necessary in a society where every act proclaims them), but that it provides a metasocial commentary upon the whole matter of assorting human beings into fixed hierarchical ranks and then organizing the major part of collective existence around that assortment. Its function, if you want to call it that, is interpretive: it is a Balinese reading of Balinese experience; a story they tell themselves about themselves.")]

#zh(id: "S104")[#text("使斗鸡区别于日常生活、将其从日常实际事务领域中抽离出来、并赋予它更大重要性的光环的，并不是像功能主义社会学所认为的那样，它强化了地位歧视（在一个每项行为都宣扬地位歧视的社会中，这种强化几乎是不必要的），而是它为将人类划分为固定的等级等级，然后围绕这种分类组织集体存在的主要部分的整个问题提供了一种元社会评论。如果你想这样称呼它的话，它的功能是解释性的：它是对巴厘岛经验的巴厘岛式解读；他们讲述自己的故事。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S105")[#text("To put the matter this way is to engage in a bit of metaphorical refocusing of one’s own, for it shifts the analysis of cultural forms from an endeavor in general parallel to dissecting an organism, diagnosing a symptom, deciphering a code, or ordering a system–the dominant analogies in contemporary anthropology–to one in general parallel with penetrating a literary text. If one takes the cockfight, or any other collectively sustained symbolic structure, as a means of “saying something of something” (to invoke a famous Aristotelian tag), then one is faced with a problem not in social mechanics but social semantics.36 For the anthropologist, whose concern is with formulating sociological principles, not with promoting or appreciating cockfights, the question is, what does one learn about such principles from examining culture as an assemblage of texts?")]

#zh(id: "S105")[#text("这样说，就是对自己的隐喻进行了重新定位，因为它将对文化形式的分析从一般与解剖有机体、诊断症状、破译密码或整理系统（当代人类学中占主导地位的类比）平行的努力转变为与洞察文学文本一般平行的分析。如果一个人将斗鸡或任何其他集体维持的象征结构视为“说某事”的一种手段（援引一个著名的亚里士多德标签），那么我们面临的问题就不是社会机制的问题，而是社会语义学的问题。 36对于人类学家来说，他们关心的是制定社会学原理，而不是促进或欣赏斗鸡，问题是，通过将文化作为文本的集合来考察，人们可以从这些原则中学到什么？")]

#html.p(id: "en-S106")[#text("Such an extension of the notion of a text beyond written material, and even beyond verbal, is, though metaphorical, not, of course, all that novel. The interpretatio naturae tradition of the Middle Ages, which, culminating in Spinoza, attempted to read nature as Scripture, the Nietszchean effort to treat value systems as glosses on the will to power (or the Marxian one to treat them as glosses on property relations), and the Freudian replacement of the enigmatic text of the manifest dream with the plain one of the latent, all offer precedents, if not equally recommendable ones.37 But the idea remains theoretically undeveloped; and the more profound corollary, so far as anthropology is concerned, that cultural forms can be treated as texts, as imaginative works built out of social materials, has yet to be systematically exploited.38")]

#zh(id: "S106")[#text("文本概念的这种延伸超出了书面材料，甚至超出了口头材料，尽管是隐喻性的，但当然并不是那么新颖。中世纪的自然解释传统，在斯宾诺莎试图将自然解读为《圣经》，这一传统在斯宾诺莎那里达到顶峰，尼采试图将价值体系视为对权力意志的注解（或者马克思主义将其视为对财产关系的注解），以及弗洛伊德用朴素的潜在文本取代显梦的神秘文本，所有这些都提供了先例，即使不是同样值得推荐的先例。 37但这一想法在理论上仍然没有发展；就人类学而言，更深刻的推论，即文化形式可以被视为文本，被视为由社会材料构建的富有想象力的作品，尚未得到系统的利用。 38")]

#html.p(id: "en-S107")[#text("In the case at hand, to treat the cockfight as a text is to bring out a feature of it (in my opinion, the central feature of it) that treating it as a rite or a pastime, the two most obvious alternatives, would tend to obscure: its use of emotion for cognitive ends. What the cockfight says it says in a vocabulary of sentiment–the thrill of risk, the despair of loss, the pleasure of triumph. Yet what it says is not merely that risk is exciting, loss depressing, or triumph gratifying, banal tautologies of affect, but that it is of these emotions, thus exampled, that society is built and individuals put together. Attending cockfights and participating in them is, for the Balinese, a kind of sentimental education. What he learns there is what his culture’s ethos and his private sensibility (or, anyway, certain aspects of them) look like when spelled out externally in a collective text; that the two are near enough alike to be articulated in the symbolics of a single such text; and–the disquieting part–that the text in which this revelation is accomplished consists of a")]

#zh(id: "S107")[#text("就目前的情况而言，将斗鸡视为一种文本，是为了凸显它的一个特征（在我看来，这是它的核心特征），即把它视为一种仪式或一种消遣，这两个最明显的选择，往往会变得模糊：它利用情感来达到认知目的。斗鸡所表达的情感是一种情感词汇——冒险的兴奋、失败的绝望、胜利的喜悦。然而，它所说的不仅仅是风险令人兴奋，损失令人沮丧，或胜利令人欣慰，情感的平庸同义反复，而且正是这些情感，如此例证，社会得以建立，个人聚集在一起。观看斗鸡并参与其中，对于巴厘岛人来说，是一种情感教育。他在那里学到的是他的文化的精神和他的私人情感（或者，无论如何，它们的某些方面）在集体文本中外部阐明时的样子；两者非常相似，可以用一个这样的文本的象征意义来表达；并且——令人不安的部分——完成这一启示的文本由")]

#html.p(id: "en-S108")[#text("Every people, the proverb has it, loves its own form of violence. The cockfight is the Balinese reflection on theirs: on its look, its uses, its force, its fascination. Drawing on almost every level of Balinese experience, it brings together themes–animal savagery, male narcissism, opponent gambling, status rivalry, mass excitement, blood sacrifice– whose main connection is their involvement with rage and the fear of rage, and, binding them into a set of rules which at once contains them and allows them play, builds a symbolic structure in which, over and over again, the reality of their inner affiliation can be intelligibly felt. If, to quote Northrop Frye again, we go to see Macbeth to learn what a man feels like after he has gained a kingdom and lost his soul, Balinese go to cockfights to find out what a man, usually composed, aloof, almost obsessively self-absorbed, a kind of moral autocosm, feels like when, attacked, tormented, challenged, insulted, and driven in result to the extremes of fury, he has totally triumphed or been brought totally low. The whole passage, as it takes us back to Aristotle (though to the Poetics rather than the Hermeneutics), is worth quotation:")]

#zh(id: "S108")[#text("俗话说，每个民族都喜欢自己的暴力形式。斗鸡是巴厘岛人对斗鸡的反思：它的外观、它的用途、它的力量和它的魅力。它借鉴了巴厘岛人几乎各个层面的经验，汇集了主题——动物的野蛮、男性自恋、对手赌博、地位竞争、大众兴奋、血腥牺牲——其主要联系是他们对愤怒的参与和对愤怒的恐惧，并将它们绑定到一套既包含它们又允许它们玩耍的规则中，构建了一个象征性结构，在这个象征性结构中，人们可以一遍又一遍地清晰地感受到他们内心归属的现实。如果，再次引用诺斯罗普·弗莱的话，如果我们去看麦克白，了解一个人在获得王国并失去灵魂后的感受，那么巴厘岛人去斗鸡，了解一个通常沉着、冷漠、几乎痴迷于自我中心、一种道德自体的人，在受到攻击、折磨、挑战、侮辱，并最终被逼到极端愤怒时，他的感觉是怎样的，他已经完全胜利或被带入了。完全低。整段话将我们带回到亚里士多德（尽管是诗学而不是诠释学），值得引用：")]

#html.p(id: "en-S109")[#text("But the poet [as opposed to the historian], Aristotle says, never makes any real statements at all, certainly no particular or specific ones. The poet’s job is not to tell you what happened, but what happens: not what did take place, but the kind of thing that always does take place. He gives you the typical, recurring, or what Aristotle calls universal event. You wouldn’t go to Macbeth to learn about the history of Scotland–you go to it to learn what a man feels like after he’s gained a kingdom and lost his soul. When you meet")]

#zh(id: "S109")[#text("但亚里士多德说，诗人（相对于历史学家）从来没有做出任何真实的陈述，当然也没有具体的或具体的陈述。诗人的工作不是告诉你发生了什么，而是告诉你发生了什么：不是已经发生了什么，而是总是发生的事情。他为你提供了典型的、反复出现的、或者亚里士多德所说的普遍事件。你不会去《麦克白》了解苏格兰的历史——你去它是为了了解一个人在获得王国并失去灵魂后的感受。当你遇见")]

#html.p(id: "en-S110")[#text("such a character as Micawber in Dickens, you don’t feel that there must have been a man Dickens knew who was exactly like this: you feel that there’s a bit of Micawber in almost everybody you know, including yourself. Our impressions of human life are picked up one by one, and remain for most of us loose and disorganized. But we constantly find things in literature that suddenly co-ordinate and bring into focus a great many such impressions, and this is part of what Aristotle means by the typical or universal human event.39")]

#zh(id: "S110")[#text("狄更斯笔下的米考伯这样的人物，你不会觉得狄更斯认识的人一定是这样的：你觉得你认识的几乎每个人，包括你自己，都有一点米考伯的影子。我们对人类生活的印象是一一积累的，对我们大多数人来说仍然是松散和杂乱的。但我们不断地在文学中发现一些东西，这些东西突然协调并集中了许多这样的印象，这就是亚里士多德所说的典型或普遍的人类事件的一部分。 39")]

#html.p(id: "en-S111")[#text("It is this kind of bringing of assorted experiences of everyday life to focus that the cockfight, set aside from that life as “only a game” and reconnected to it as “more than a game,” accomplishes, and so creates what, better than typical or universal, could be called a paradigmatic human event– that is, one that tells us less what happens than the kind of thing that would happen if, as is not the case, life were art and could be as freely shaped by styles of feeling as Macbeth and David Copperfield are.")]

#zh(id: "S111")[#text("正是这种将日常生活中的各种经历集中在一起的方式，使斗鸡脱离了生活，将其视为“只是一场游戏”，并将其重新连接为“不仅仅是一场游戏”，从而创造了比典型或普遍的更好的人类事件范例——也就是说，它告诉我们的不是发生了什么，而是如果生活是艺术，并且可以像麦克白和大卫那样被感觉风格自由塑造，而不是告诉我们会发生什么事情。科波菲尔是。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S112")[#text("Enacted and reenacted, so far without end, the cockfight enables the Balinese, as, read and reread, Macbeth enables us, to see a dimension of his own subjectivity. As he watches fight after fight with the active watching of an owner and a bettor (for cockfighting has no more interest as a pure spectator sport than croquet or dog racing do), he grows familiar with it and what it has to say to him, much as the attentive listener to string quartets or the absorbed viewer of still lifes grows slowly more familiar with them in a way which opens his subjectivity to himself.40")]

#zh(id: "S112")[#text("斗鸡一再上演，至今永无休止，它让巴厘岛人看到了他自己主观性的一个维度，就像读了又重读的《麦克白》一样，它让我们看到了他自己的主观性。当他在主人和投注者的积极观看下观看一场又一场的战斗时（因为斗鸡作为一种纯粹的观赏性运动，并不比槌球或赛狗更有兴趣），他逐渐熟悉了它以及它对他说的话，就像弦乐四重奏的细心聆听者或静物画的全神贯注的观众以一种向自己开放主观性的方式慢慢地更加熟悉它们一样。 40")]

#html.p(id: "en-S113")[#text("Yet, because–in another of those paradoxes, along with painted feelings and unconsequenced acts, which haunt aesthetics–that subjectivity does not properly exist until it is thus organized, art forms generate and regenerate the very subjectivity they pretend only to display. Quartets, still lifes, and cockfights are not merely reflections of a preexisting sensibility analogically represented; they are positive agents in the creation and maintenance of such a sensibility. If we see ourselves as a pack of Micawbers it is from reading too much Dickens (if we see ourselves as unillusioned realists, it is from reading too little); and similarly for Balinese, cocks, and cockfights. It is in such a way, coloring experience with the light they cast it in, rather than through whatever material effects they may have, that the arts play their role, as arts, in social life.41")]

#zh(id: "S113")[#text("然而，因为——在另一个悖论中，连同描绘的情感和无关紧要的行为，困扰着美学——主观性只有在如此组织起来之后才能正确存在，艺术形式产生并再生了它们假装只是展示的主观性。四重奏、静物画和斗鸡不仅是类比代表的先前存在的情感的反映；它们是创造和维持这种情感的积极推动者。如果我们把自己视为一群米考伯，那是因为读了太多狄更斯的作品（如果我们把自己视为不抱幻想的现实主义者，那是因为读得太少了）；巴厘岛人、公鸡和斗鸡也是如此。正是通过这样一种方式，艺术通过它们所投射的光线来着色经验，而不是通过它们可能具有的任何物质效果，在社会生活中发挥其作为艺术的作用。 41")]

#html.p(id: "en-S114")[#text("In the cockfight, then, the Balinese forms and discovers his temperament and his society’s temper at the same time. Or, more exactly, he forms and discovers a particular face of them. Not only are there a great many other cultural texts providing commentaries on status hierarchy and self-regard in Bali, but there are a great many other critical sectors of Balinese life besides the stratificatory and the agonistic that receive such commentary. The ceremony consecrating a Brahmana priest, a matter of breath control, postural immobility, and vacant concentration upon the depths of being, displays a radically different, but to the Balinese equally real, property of social hierarchy–its reach toward the numinous transcendent. Set not in the matrix of the kinetic emotionality of animals, but in that of the static passionlessness of divine mentality, it expresses tranquility not disquiet. The mass festivals at the village temples, which mobilize the whole local population in elaborate hostings of visiting gods–songs, dances, compliments, gifts–assert the spiritual unity of village mates against their status inequality and project a mood of amity and trust.42 The cockfight is not the master key to Balinese life, any more than bullfighting is to Spanish. What it says about that life is not unqualified nor even unchallenged by what other equally eloquent cultural statements say about it. But there is nothing more surprising in this than in the fact that Racine and Molière were contemporaries, or that the same people who arrange chrysanthemums cast swords.43")]

#zh(id: "S114")[#text("那么，在斗鸡中，巴厘岛人同时形成并发现了自己的气质和社会脾气。或者，更准确地说，他形成并发现了它们的特定面孔。不仅有许多其他文化文本提供了对巴厘岛地位等级和自尊的评论，而且除了分层和竞争之外，巴厘岛人生活的许多其他关键领域也接受了此类评论。婆罗门祭司的祝圣仪式，涉及呼吸控制、姿势不动以及对存在深处的空虚专注，展示了一种截然不同的、但对巴厘岛人来说同样真实的社会等级属性——它对超然的神圣影响。它不是以动物的动态情感为母体，而是以神圣心态的静态无激情为母体，它表达的是宁静而不是不安。村庄寺庙里的群众节日动员全体当地居民精心招待来访的神灵——歌曲、舞蹈、赞美、礼物——维护了村里成员的精神团结，反对他们的地位不平等，并表现出一种友好和信任的气氛。 42斗鸡不是巴厘岛人生活的万能钥匙，就像斗牛不是西班牙人的万能钥匙一样。它对生命的描述并非没有资格，甚至不受其他同样雄辩的文化陈述的质疑。但最令人惊讶的莫过于拉辛和莫里哀是同时代人，或者是同一群安排菊花的人铸造了剑。 43")]

#html.p(id: "en-S115")[#text("The culture of a people is an ensemble of texts, themselves ensembles, which the anthropologist strains to read over the shoulders of those to whom they properly belong. There are enormous difficulties in such an enterprise, methodological pitfalls to make a Freudian quake, and some moral perplexities as well. Nor is it the only way that symbolic forms can be sociologically handled. Functionalism lives, and so does psychologism. But to regard such forms as “saying something of something,” and saying it to somebody, is at least to open up the possibility of an analysis which attends to their substance rather than to reductive formulas professing to account for them.")]

#zh(id: "S115")[#text("一个民族的文化是文本的集合，其本身就是集合，人类学家竭尽全力地在他们所属的人的肩膀上阅读它们。这样的事业存在着巨大的困难，方法论上的陷阱足以引发弗洛伊德式的震动，而且还存在一些道德上的困惑。这也不是社会学处理符号形式的唯一方法。功能主义依然存在，心理主义也同样存在。但是，将这些形式视为“对某事说某事”，并向某人说，至少开启了一种分析的可能性，这种分析关注它们的实质，而不是声称可以解释它们的简化公式。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S116")[#text("As in more familiar exercises in close reading, one can start anywhere in a culture’s repertoire of forms and end up anywhere else. One can stay, as I have here, within a single, more or less bounded form and circle steadily within it. One can move between forms in search of broader unities or informing contrasts. One can even compare forms from different cultures to define their character in reciprocal relief. But whatever the level at which one operates, and however intricately, the guiding principle is the same: societies, like lives, contain their own interpretations. One has only to learn how to gain access to them.")]

#zh(id: "S116")[#text("正如在更熟悉的细读练习中一样，人们可以从一种文化的形式库中的任何地方开始，然后以其他地方结束。就像我在这里一样，一个人可以停留在一个单一的、或多或少有限制的形式内，并在其中稳定地循环。人们可以在各种形式之间移动，以寻找更广泛的统一性或鲜明的对比。人们甚至可以比较不同文化的形式，以相互理解的方式来定义它们的特征。但无论一个人的运作水平如何，无论多么复杂，指导原则都是相同的：社会就像生活一样，有自己的解释。人们只需学习如何获得它们。")]

