#html.p(id: "en-N002")[#text("2 Jane Belo, “The Balinese Temper,” in Jane Belo, ed., Traditional Balinese Culture (New York: Columbia University Press, 1970; originally published in 1935), 85–110.")]

#zh(id: "N002")[#text("2 Jane Belo，“巴厘岛人的脾气”，载于 Jane Belo 主编，传统巴厘岛文化（纽约：哥伦比亚大学出版社，1970 年；最初出版于 1935 年），85-110。")]

#html.p(id: "en-N003")[#text("3 The best discussion of cockfighting is again Bateson and Mead’s (Balinese Character, 24–25, 140), but it, too, is general and abbreviated.")]

#zh(id: "N003")[#text("3 对斗鸡最好的讨论还是贝特森和米德的（巴厘岛性格，24-25, 140），但它也是笼统而简短的。")]

#html.p(id: "en-N004")[#text("4 Ibid., 25–26. The cockfight is unusual within Balinese culture in being a single sex public activity from which the other sex is totally and expressly excluded. Sexual differentiation is culturally extremely played down in Bali and most activities, formal and informal, involve the participation of men and women on equal ground, commonly as linked couples. From religion, to politics, to economics, to kinship, to dress, Bali is a rather “unisex” society, a fact both its customs and its symbolism clearly express. Even in contexts where women do not in fact play much of a role–music, painting, certain agricultural activities–their absence, which is only relative in any case, is more a mere matter of fact than socially enforced. To this general pattern, the cockfight, entirely of, by, and for men (women–at least Balinese women–do not even watch), is the most striking exception.")]

#zh(id: "N004")[#text("4 同上，25-26。斗鸡在巴厘岛文化中很不寻常，因为它是一种单一性别的公共活动，异性完全被明确排除在外。在巴厘岛，性别差异在文化上被极度淡化，大多数正式和非正式的活动都涉及男性和女性在平等的基础上参与，通常是作为相连的夫妻。从宗教、政治、经济、亲属关系到服饰，巴厘岛是一个相当“男女皆宜”的社会，其习俗和象征意义都清楚地表达了这一事实。即使在妇女实际上并没有发挥多大作用的环境中——音乐、绘画、某些农业活动——她们的缺席在任何情况下都只是相对的，更多的是一个事实问题，而不是社会强制的。相对于这种普遍模式，斗鸡是最引人注目的例外，它完全由男性主导、由男性主导、为男性服务（女性——至少是巴厘岛女性——甚至不观看）。")]

#html.p(id: "en-N005")[#text("5 Christiaan Hooykaas, The Lay of the Jaya Prana (London: Luzac, 1958), 39. The lay has a stanza (no. 17) with the reluctant bridegroom use. Jaya Prana, the subject of a Balinese Uriah myth, responds to the lord who has offered him the loveliest of six hundred servant girls: “Godly King, my Lord and Master / I beg you, give me leave to go / such things are not yet in my mind; / like a fighting cock encaged / indeed I am on my mettle / I am alone / as yet the flame has not been fanned.”")]

#zh(id: "N005")[#text("5 Christiaan Hooykaas, The Lay of the Jaya Prana (London: Luzac, 1958), 39。 居士有一节（第 17 节）是不情愿的新郎使用的。巴厘岛乌利亚神话的主题贾亚·普拉纳 (Jaya Prana) 回应为他提供六百名最可爱的女仆的领主：“神圣的国王，我的主和主人/我恳求您允许我离开/这些事情还没有出现在我的脑海中；/就像一只被关在笼子里的斗鸡/事实上，我充满勇气/我独自一人/火焰还没有被煽动。”")]

#html.p(id: "en-N006")[#text("6 For these, see V. E. Korn, Het Adatrecht van Bali, 2d ed. (s’Gravenhage: G. Naeff, 1932), index under toh.")]

#zh(id: "N006")[#text("6 有关这些内容，请参见 V. E. Korn，Het Adatrecht van Bali，第 2 版。 （s’Gravenhage：G. Naeff，1932），索引在 toh 下。")]

#html.p(id: "en-N007")[#text("7 There is indeed a legend to the effect that the separation of Java and Bali is due to the action of a powerful Javanese religious figure who wished to protect himself against a Balinese culture hero (the ancestor of two Ksatria castes) who was a passionate cockfighting gambler. See Christiaan Hooykaas, Agama Tirtha (Amsterdam: Noord-Hollandsche, 1964), 184.")]

#zh(id: "N007")[#text("7 确实有一个传说，大意是爪哇和巴厘岛的分离是由于一位强大的爪哇宗教人物的行动，他希望保护自己免受巴厘岛文化英雄（两个刹帝利种姓的祖先）的侵害，而这位英雄是一位狂热的斗鸡赌徒。参见 Christiaan Hooykaas，Agama Tirtha（阿姆斯特丹：Noord-Hollandsche，1964），184。")]

#html.p(id: "en-N008")[#text("8 An incestuous couple is forced to wear pig yokes over their necks and crawl to a pig trough and eat with their mouths there. On this, see Jane Belo, “Customs Pertaining to Twins in Bali,” in Belo, ed., Traditional Balinese Culture, 49; on the abhorrence of animality generally, Bateson and Mead, Balinese Character, 22.")]

#zh(id: "N008")[#text("8 一对乱伦夫妇被迫在脖子上套上猪轭，爬到猪槽边用嘴吃东西。关于这一点，请参阅 Jane Belo，“巴厘岛双胞胎的习俗”，载于 Belo 编辑，传统巴厘岛文化，49；关于普遍对动物性的憎恶，贝特森和米德，巴厘岛性格，22。")]

#html.p(id: "en-N009")[#text("9 Except for unimportant, small-bet fights (on the question of fight “importance,” see below) spur affixing is usually done by someone other than the owner. Whether the owner handles his own cock or not more or less depends on how skilled he is at it, a consideration whose importance is again relative to the importance of the fight. When spur affixers and cock handlers are someone other than the owner, they are almost always a quite close relative–a brother or cousin–or a very intimate friend of his. They are thus almost extensions of his personality, as the fact that all three will refer to the cock as “mine,” say “I” fought So-and-So, and so on, demonstrates. Also, owner-handler-affixer triads tend to be fairly fixed, though individuals may participate in several and often exchange roles within a given one.")]

#zh(id: "N009")[#text("9 除了不重要的小额打斗（关于打斗“重要性”的问题，见下文）外，固定马刺通常是由除主人以外的其他人进行的。主人是否处理自己的鸡鸡或多或少取决于他的熟练程度，这一考虑的重要性又与战斗的重要性相关。当马刺固定者和公鸡处理者不是主人时，他们几乎总是他的近亲——兄弟或表兄弟——或者是他的非常亲密的朋友。因此，它们几乎是他个性的延伸，事实证明，这三个人都会将公鸡称为“我的”，说“我”与某某打斗，等等。此外，所有者-处理者-固定者三合会往往是相当固定的，尽管个人可能会参与多个角色，并且经常在一个给定的角色中交换角色。")]

#html.p(id: "en-N010")[#text("10 Erving Goffman, Encounters: Two Studies in the Sociology of Interaction (Indianapolis: Bobbs- Merrill, 1961), 9–10.")]

#zh(id: "N010")[#text("10 欧文·戈夫曼（Erving Goffman），《遭遇：互动社会学的两项研究》（印第安纳波利斯：鲍布斯-梅里尔，1961 年），9-10。")]

#html.p(id: "en-N011")[#text("11 This word, which literally means an indelible stain or mark, as in a birthmark or a vein in a stone, is used as well for a deposit in a court case, for a pawn, for security offered in a loan, for a stand-in for someone else in a legal or ceremonial context, for an earnest advanced in a business deal, for a sign placed in a field to indicate its ownership is in dispute, and for the status of an unfaithful wife from whose lover her husband must gain satisfaction or surrender her to him. See Korn, Het Adatrecht van Bali; Theodoor Pigeaud, Javaans-Nederlands Handwoordenboek (Groningen: Wolters, 1938); H. H. Juynboll, Oudjavaansche-Nederlandsche Woordenlijst (Leiden: Brill, 1923).")]

#zh(id: "N011")[#text("11 这个词的字面意思是不可磨灭的污点或印记，如胎记或石头上的静脉，也可用于法庭案件中的押金、典当、贷款中提供的担保、法律或礼仪场合中的其他人的替身、商业交易中的真诚预付款、放置在田野上以表明其所有权存在争议的标志，以及不忠的妻子的地位，她的丈夫必须从其情人那里获得满足或满意。将她交给他。参见 Korn，Het Adatrecht van Bali； Theodoor Pigeaud，Javaans-Nederlands Handwoordenboek（格罗宁根：Wolters，1938）； H. H. Juynboll，《Oudjavaansche-Nederlandsche Woordenlijst》（莱顿：布里尔，1923 年）。")]

#html.p(id: "en-N012")[#text("12 The center bet must be advanced in cash by both parties prior to the actual fight. The umpire holds the stakes until the decision is rendered and then awards them to the winner, avoiding, among other things, the intense embarrassment both winner and loser would feel if the latter had to pay off personally following his defeat. About 10 percent of the winner’s receipts are subtracted for the umpire’s share and that of the fight sponsors.")]

#zh(id: "N012")[#text("12 中间投注必须由双方在实际比赛前以现金预付。裁判员持有赌注，直到做出决定，然后将赌注奖励给获胜者，这样可以避免获胜者和失败者在失败后必须亲自偿还时感到的强烈尴尬。获胜者收入的大约 10% 会扣除裁判员和比赛赞助商的份额。")]

#html.p(id: "en-N013")[#text("13 Actually, the typing of cocks, which is extremely elaborate (I have collected more than twenty classes, certainly not a complete list), is not based on color alone, but on a series of independent, interacting, dimensions, which include, beside color, size, bone thickness, plumage, and temperament. (But not pedigree. The Balinese do not breed cocks to any significant extent, nor, so far as I have been able to discover, have they ever done so. The asil, or jungle cock, which is the basic fighting strain everywhere the sport is found, is native to southern Asia, and one can buy a good example in the chicken section of almost any Balinese market for anywhere from 4 or 5 ringgits up to 50 or more.) The color element is merely the one normally used as the type name, except when the two cocks of different types–as on principle they must be–have the same color, in which case a secondary indication from one of the other dimensions (“large speckled” v. “small speckled,” etc.) is added. The types are coordinated with various cosmological ideas which help shape the making of matches, so that, for example, you fight a small, headstrong, speckled brown-on-white cock with flat-lying feathers and thin legs from the east side of the ring on a certain day of the complex Balinese calendar, and a large, cautious, all-black cock with tufted feathers and stubby legs from the north side on another day, and so on. All this is again recorded in palm-leaf manuscripts and endlessly discussed by the Balinese (who do not all have identical systems), and full-scale componential-cum-symbolic analysis of cock classifications would be extremely valuable both as an adjunct to the description of the cockfight and in itself. But my data on the subject, though extensive and varied, do not seem to be complete and systematic enough to attempt such an analysis here. For Balinese cosmological ideas more generally see Belo, ed., Traditional Balinese Culture, and J. L. Swellengrebel, ed., Bali: Studies in Life, Thought, and Ritual (The Hague: W. van Hoeve, 1960); for calendrical ones, Clifford Geertz, Person, Time, and Conduct in Bali: An Essay in Cultural Analysis (New Haven, Conn.: Southeast Asia Studies, Yale University, 1966), 45–53.")]

#zh(id: "N013")[#text("13 事实上，公鸡的分类是极其复杂的（我收集了二十多个类别，当然不是完整的列表），它不仅仅基于颜色，而是基于一系列独立的、相互作用的维度，除了颜色之外，还包括大小、骨骼厚度、羽毛和气质。 （但不是血统。巴厘岛人没有在很大程度上饲养公鸡，据我所知，他们也没有这样做过。丛林公鸡是这项运动随处可见的基本格斗品系，原产于南亚，人们可以在几乎任何巴厘岛市场的鸡肉区买到一只很好的例子，价格从 4 或 5 林吉特到 50 林吉特或更多。）颜色元素只是通常用作类型的元素。名称，除非两只不同类型的公鸡（原则上它们必须如此）具有相同的颜色，在这种情况下，添加来自其他维度之一的辅助指示（“大斑点”与“小斑点”等）。这些类型与有助于塑造比赛制作的各种宇宙学思想相协调，因此，例如，您可以在复杂的巴厘岛日历的某一天与来自环东侧的一只小而任性、有斑点的棕白公鸡进行战斗，这只公鸡具有平躺的羽毛和细腿，而在另一天与来自北侧的一只大而谨慎的全黑公鸡进行战斗，它有簇状羽毛和粗短的腿，等等。所有这些都再次记录在棕榈叶手稿中，并被巴厘岛人（他们并不都有相同的系统）无休止地讨论，而对公鸡分类的全面成分和符号分析将非常有价值，无论是作为斗鸡描述的辅助还是斗鸡本身。但我关于这个主题的数据虽然广泛且多样，但似乎并不足够完整和系统，可以在这里尝试这样的分析。对于巴厘岛的宇宙论思想，请参阅 Belo 编辑的《传统巴厘岛文化》和 J. L. Swellengrebel 编辑的《巴厘岛：生活、思想和仪式研究》（海牙：W. van Hoeve，1960 年）；对于日历，Clifford Geertz，巴厘岛的人物、时间和行为：文化分析论文（康涅狄格州纽黑文：东南亚研究，耶鲁大学，1966 年），45-53。")]

#html.p(id: "en-N014")[#text("14 For purposes of ethnographic completeness, it should be noted that it is possible for the man backing the favorite–the odds-giver –to make a bet in which he wins if his cock wins or there is a tie, a slight shortening of the odds (I do not have enough cases to be exact, but ties seem to occur about once every fifteen or twenty matches). He indicates his wish to do this by shouting sapih (“tie”) rather than the cock-type, but such bets are in fact infrequent.")]

#zh(id: "N014")[#text("14 出于人种学完整性的目的，应该指出的是，支持热门球队的人（赔率提供者）可以下注，如果他的雄鸽获胜或出现平局，则他获胜，赔率略有缩短（我没有足够的案例来准确说明，但平局似乎大约每十五或二十场比赛就会发生一次）。他通过喊“sapih”（“领带”）而不是“公鸡”来表达他的愿望，但实际上这样的赌注并不常见。")]

#html.p(id: "en-N015")[#text("15 The precise dynamics of the movement of the betting is one of the most intriguing, most complicated, and, given the hectic conditions under which it occurs, most difficult to study, aspects of the fight. Motion picture recording plus multiple observers would probably be necessary to deal with it effectively. Even impressionistically–the only approach open to a lone ethnographer caught in the middle of all this– it is clear that certain men lead both in determining the favorite (that is, making the opening cock-type calls which always initiate the process) and in directing the movement of the odds, these “opinion leaders” being the more accomplished cockfighters-cum-solid-citizens to be discussed below. If these men begin to change their calls, others follow; if they begin to make bets, so do others and–though there is always a large number of frustrated bettors crying for shorter or longer odds to the end– the movement more or less ceases. But a detailed understanding of the whole process awaits what, alas, it is not very likely ever to get: a decision theorist armed with precise observations of individual behavior.")]

#zh(id: "N015")[#text("15 投注运动的精确动态是战斗中最有趣、最复杂的方面之一，而且考虑到比赛发生的繁忙条件，也是最难研究的方面。为了有效地处理这个问题，可能需要动态图像录制加上多个观察者。即使是印象派——这是对陷入这一切的孤独的民族志学者开放的唯一方法——也很明显，某些人在确定最喜欢的人（即，总是发出公鸡式的叫声，总是启动这个过程）和指导赔率的变化方面都处于领先地位，这些“意见领袖”是下面讨论的更有成就的斗鸡士兼固体公民。如果这些人开始改变他们的主张，其他人也会效仿。如果他们开始下注，其他人也开始下注，尽管总是有大量沮丧的下注者哭着要求更短或更长的赔率到最后，但这种运动或多或少会停止。但对整个过程的详细理解等待着一个可惜的是不太可能得到的东西：一个对个人行为进行精确观察的决策理论家。")]

#html.p(id: "en-N016")[#text("16 Assuming only binomial variability, the departure from a fifty-fifty expectation in the 60 ringgits and below case is 1.38 standard deviations, or (in a one-direction test) an 8 in 100 possibility by chance alone; for the below 40 ringgits case it is 1.65 standard deviations, or about 5 in 100. The fact that these departures though real are not extreme merely indicates, again, that even in the smaller fights the tendency to match cocks at least reasonably evenly persists. It is a matter of relative relaxation of the pressures toward equalization, not their elimination. The tendency for high-bet contests to be coin-flip propositions is, of course, even more striking, and suggests the Balinese know quite well what they are about.")]

#zh(id: "N016")[#text("16 假设只有二项式变异，在 60 林吉特及以下的情况下，与 550 的期望的偏离是 1.38 个标准差，或（在单向测试中）仅偶然的可能性就有 100 分之 8；对于低于 40 林吉特的情况，这一比例为 1.65 个标准差，或大约为 100 分之 5。事实是，这些偏差虽然不是极端的，但再次表明，即使在规模较小的比赛中，公鸡匹配的趋势至少仍然相当均匀。这是一个相对缓解平等压力的问题，而不是消除压力的问题。当然，高赌注竞赛以抛硬币形式进行的趋势更为引人注目，这表明巴厘岛人非常了解它们的含义。")]

#html.p(id: "en-N017")[#text("17 The reduction in wagering in smaller fights (which, of course, feeds on itself; one of the reasons people find small fights uninteresting is that there is less wagering in them, and contrariwise for large ones) takes place in three mutually reinforcing ways. First, there is a simple withdrawal of interest as people wander off to have a cup of coffee or chat with a friend. Second, the Balinese do not mathematically reduce odds, but bet directly in terms of stated odds as such. Thus, for a 9–8 bet, one man wagers 9 ringgits, the other 8; for 5–4, one wagers 5, the other 4. For any given currency unit, like the ringgit, therefore, 6.3 times as much money is involved in a 10–9 bet as in a 2–1 bet, for example, and, as noted, in small fights betting settles toward the longer end. Finally, the bets which are made tend to be one- rather than two-, three-, or in some of the very largest fights, four- or five-finger ones. (The fingers indicate the multiples of the stated bet odds at issue, not absolute figures. Two fingers in a 6–5 situation means a man wants to wager 10 ringgits on the underdog against 12, three in an 8–7 situation, 21 against 24, and so on.)")]

#zh(id: "N017")[#text("17 小型拳击比赛中投注额的减少（当然，这会自我调节；人们觉得小型拳击比赛无趣的原因之一是小型拳击比赛中的投注额较少，大型拳击比赛则相反）以三种相辅相成的方式发生。首先，当人们去喝杯咖啡或与朋友聊天时，就会失去兴趣。其次，巴厘岛人不会从数学上降低赔率，而是直接按照规定的赔率进行投注。因此，对于 9-8 的投注，一名男子投注 9 令吉，另一名男子投注 8 令吉；例如，对于 5-4，一个人下注 5，另一个下注 4。因此，对于任何给定的货币单位（如林吉特），10-9 投注所涉及的资金是 2-1 投注的 6.3 倍，并且，如前所述，在小规模战斗中，投注会倾向于长期结算。最后，所下的赌注往往是一指，而不是两指、三指，或者在一些规模最大的比赛中，四指或五指下注。 （手指表示有争议的投注赔率的倍数，而不是绝对数字。在 6-5 的情况下，两根手指意味着一个人想在失败者对 12 人下注 10 林吉特，在 8-7 情况下下注 3 林吉特，在 21 对 24 情况下下注 21 林吉特，依此类推。）")]

#html.p(id: "en-N018")[#text("18 Besides wagering there are other economic aspects of the cockfight, especially its very close connection with the local market system which, though secondary both to its motivation and to its function, are not without importance. Cockfights are open events to which anyone who wishes may come, sometimes from quite distant areas, but well over 90 percent, probably over 95, are very local affairs, and the locality concerned is defined not by the village, nor even by the administrative district, but by the rural market system. Bali has a three-day market week with the familiar “solar-system” type rotation. Though the markets themselves have never been very highly developed, small morning affairs in a village square, it is the microregion such rotation rather generally marks out –ten or twenty square miles, seven or eight neighboring villages (which in contemporary Bali is usually going to mean anywhere from five to ten or eleven thousand people) from which the core of any cockfight audience, indeed virtually all of it, will come. Most of the fights are in fact organized and sponsored by small combines of petty rural merchants under the general premise, very strongly held by them and indeed by all Balinese, that cockfights are good for trade because “they get money out of the house, they make it circulate.” Stalls selling various sorts of things as well as assorted sheerchance gambling games (see below) are set up around the edge of the area so that this even takes on the quality of a small fair. This connection of cockfighting with markets and market sellers is very old, as, among other things, their conjunction in inscriptions (Roelof Goris, Prasasti Bali, 2 vols. [Bandung: N. V. Masa Baru, 1954]) indicates. Trade has followed the cock for centuries in rural Bali and the sport has been one of the main agencies of the island’s monetization.")]

#zh(id: "N018")[#text("18 除了投注之外，斗鸡还有其他经济方面，特别是它与当地市场体系的密切联系，尽管其动机和功能都是次要的，但并非没有重要性。斗鸡是公开活动，任何愿意参加的人都可以参加，有时来自很远的地区，但百分之九十以上，甚至可能超过百分之九十五，都是非常地方性的活动，而且所涉及的地方不是由村庄，甚至不是由行政区划定的，而是由农村市场体系来划定的。巴厘岛有为期三天的市场周，具有熟悉的“太阳系”式轮换。尽管市场本身从来都不是高度发达的，在村庄广场上举行的小型晨间活动，但这种轮换通常标志着一个小区域——十或二十平方英里，七八个邻近的村庄（在当代巴厘岛通常意味着五到一万或一万一千人），任何斗鸡观众的核心，实际上是所有斗鸡观众，都将来自这里。事实上，大多数斗鸡活动都是由农村小商人的小型联合体组织和赞助的，其总体前提是斗鸡有利于贸易，因为“斗鸡可以从家里赚钱，可以流通”，他们甚至所有巴厘岛人都强烈支持斗鸡。出售各种物品的摊位以及各种赌博游戏（见下文）都设置在该区域的边缘，因此甚至具有小型集市的质量。斗鸡与市场和市场卖家的这种联系非常古老，除其他外，它们在铭文中的结合（Roelof Goris，Prasasti Bali，2 卷。[万隆：N.V.Masa Baru，1954]）表明了这一点。几个世纪以来，巴厘岛乡村的贸易一直跟随公鸡的脚步这项运动一直是该岛货币化的主要机构之一。")]

