#html.p(id: "en-N026")[#text("26 As this is a formal paradigm, it is intended to display the logical, not the causal, structure of cockfighting. Just which of these considerations leads to which, in what order, and by what mechanisms, is another matter–one I have attempted to shed some light on in the general discussion.")]

#zh(id: "N026")[#text("26 由于这是一个正式范例，因此旨在展示斗鸡的逻辑结构，而不是因果结构。这些考虑中的哪一个导致哪一个、以什么顺序、通过什么机制，是另一回事——我试图在一般性讨论中阐明这一点。")]

#html.p(id: "en-N027")[#text("27 In another of Hooykaas-van Leeuwen Boomkamp’s folk tales (“De Gast,” Sprookjes en Verhalen van Bali, 172–180), a low caste Sudra, a generous, pious, and carefree man who is also an accomplished cockfighter, loses, despite his accomplishment, fight after fight until he is not only out of money but down to his last cock. He does not despair, however–“I bet,” he says, “upon the Unseen World.” His wife, a good and hardworking woman, knowing how much he enjoys cockfighting, gives him her last “rainy day” money to go and bet. But, filled with misgivings due to his run of ill luck, he leaves his own cock at home and bets merely on the side. He soon loses all but a coin or two and repairs to a food stand for a snack, where he meets a decrepit, odorous, and generally unappetizing old beggar leaning on a staff. The old man asks for food, and the hero spends his last coins to buy him some. The old man then asks to pass the night with the hero, which the hero gladly invites him to do. As there is no food in the house, however, the hero tells his wife to kill the last cock for dinner. When the old man discovers this fact, he tells the hero he has three cocks in his own mountain hut and says the hero may have one of them for fighting. He also asks for the hero’s son to accompany him as a servant, and, after the son agrees, this is done. The old man turns out to be Siva and, thus, to live in a great palace in the sky, though the hero does not know this. In time, the hero decides to visit his son and collect the promised cock. Lifted up into Siva’s presence, he is given the choice of three cocks. The first crows: “I have beaten fifteen opponents.” The second crows, “I have beaten twenty-five opponents.” The third crows, “I have beaten the King.” “That one, the third, is my choice,” says the hero, and returns with it to earth. When he arrives at the cockfight, he is asked for an entry fee and replies, “I have no money; I will pay after my cock has won.” As he is known never to win, he is let in because the king, who is there fighting, dislikes him and hopes to enslave him when he loses and cannot pay off. In order to ensure that this happens, the king matches his finest cock against the hero’s. When the cocks are placed down, the hero’s flees, and the crowd, led by the arrogant king, hoots in laughter. The hero’s cock then flies at the king himself, killing him with a spur stab in the throat. The hero flees. His house is encircled by the king’s men. The cock changes into a Garuda, the great mythic bird of Indic legend, and carries the hero and his wife to safety in the heavens. When the people see this, they make the hero king and his wife queen and they return as such to earth. Later their son, released by Siva, also returns and the hero-king announces his intention to enter a hermitage. (“I will fight no more cockfights. I have bet on the Unseen and won.”) He enters the hermitage and his son becomes king.")]

#zh(id: "N027")[#text("27 在 Hooykaas-van Leeuwen Boomkamp 的另一个民间故事中（“De Gast”，Sprookjes en Verhalen van Bali，172-180），一个低种姓首陀罗，一个慷慨、虔诚、无忧无虑的人，也是一位出色的斗鸡人，尽管他取得了成就，但在一场又一场的战斗中失败了，直到他不仅没钱了，而且只剩下最后一只公鸡了。然而，他并没有绝望——“我打赌，”他说，“赌在看不见的世界上。”他的妻子是一位善良而勤奋的女人，知道他有多喜欢斗鸡，所以给了他最后的“未雨绸缪”的钱去赌。但是，由于运气不好，他充满了疑虑，他把自己的鸡巴留在家里，只做旁注。很快他就失去了所有的东西，只剩下一两枚硬币，于是他来到一个小吃摊买点小吃，在那里他遇到了一个拄着拐杖的、衰弱、臭气熏天、毫无胃口的老乞丐。老人要食物，英雄用掉最后的硬币给他买了一些。然后老人要求与英雄共度一夜，英雄很高兴地邀请他这样做。然而，由于家里没有食物，英雄告诉他的妻子杀死最后一只公鸡作为晚餐。当老人发现这一事实时，他告诉英雄他自己的山间小屋里有三只公鸡，并说英雄可能拥有一只用于战斗。他还请求英雄的儿子作为仆人陪伴他，在儿子同意后，这件事就完成了。老人原来是湿婆，因此住在空中的一座宏伟宫殿里，尽管英雄不知道这一点。及时，英雄决定去看望他的儿子并收集承诺的公鸡。他被举到湿婆面前，可以选择三只雄鸽。第一只乌鸦：“我已经打败了十五个对手。”第二只乌鸦说：“我已经打败了二十五个对手。”第三只乌鸦说：“我打败了国王。” “那一个，第三个，是我的选择。”英雄说道，然后带着它回到了地球。当他到达斗鸡场时，有人向他索要入场费，他回答说：“我没有钱，等我的公鸡获胜后我再付钱。”众所周知，他永远不会赢，所以他被允许进来，因为在那里战斗的国王不喜欢他，并希望在他失败而无法偿还时奴役他。为了确保这一切发生，国王将他最好的鸡巴与英雄的配对。当公鸡被放下时，英雄逃跑了，人群在傲慢的国王的带领下发出笑声。然后英雄的阴茎飞向国王本人，用马刺刺伤了他的喉咙。英雄逃跑了。他的房子被国王的人包围了。公鸡变成了印度传说中的神鸟迦楼罗，将英雄和他的妻子带到了安全的天堂。当人们看到这一点时，他们将英雄国王和他的妻子封为女王，然后他们就这样返回了地球。后来，他们的儿子被湿婆释放，也回来了，英雄王宣布他打算进入隐居。 （“我不会再斗鸡了。我在看不见的东西上打赌并赢了。”）他进入隐居处，他的儿子成为国王。")]

#html.p(id: "en-N028")[#text("28 Addict gamblers are really less declassed (for their status is, as everyone else’s, inherited) than merely impoverished and personally disgraced. The most prominent addict gambler in my cockfight circuit was actually a very high caste satria who sold off most of his considerable lands to support his habit. Though everyone privately regarded him as a fool and worse (some, more charitable, regarded him as sick), he was publicly treated with the elaborate deference and politeness due his rank. On the independence of personal reputation and public status in Bali, see Geertz, Person, Time, and Conduct, 28–35.")]

#zh(id: "N028")[#text("28 瘾君子实际上并没有被贬低（因为他们的地位和其他人一样，是遗传的），而仅仅是贫困和个人耻辱。在我的斗鸡圈中，最著名的瘾君子实际上是一个非常高种姓的萨特里亚，他卖掉了大部分可观的土地来支持他的赌瘾。尽管每个人私下里都认为他是个傻瓜，甚至更糟（有些人比较仁慈，认为他有病），但在公开场合，他受到了由于其地位而精心尊重和礼貌的对待。关于巴厘岛个人声誉和公共地位的独立性，请参阅格尔茨，《人物、时间和行为》，28-35。")]

#html.p(id: "en-N029")[#text("29 For four, somewhat variant, treatments, see Susanne Langer, Feeling and Form (New York: Scribners, 1953); Richard Wollheim, Art and Its Objects (New York: Harper and Row, 1968); Nelson Goodman, Languages of Art (Indianapolis: Bobbs-Merrill, 1968); Maurice Merleau- Ponty, “The Eye and the Mind,” in his The Primacy of Perception (Evanston, Ill.: Northwestern University Press, 1964), 159–190.")]

#zh(id: "N029")[#text("29 对于四种略有不同的治疗方法，请参见 Susanne Langer，《Feeling and Form》（纽约：Scribners，1953 年）；理查德·沃尔海姆，《艺术及其物品》（纽约：Harper and Row，1968）；纳尔逊·古德曼，《艺术语言》（印第安纳波利斯：鲍布斯-梅里尔，1968 年）；莫里斯·梅洛-庞蒂，“眼睛与心灵”，《感知的首要性》（伊利诺伊州埃文斯顿：西北大学出版社，1964 年），159-190。")]

#html.p(id: "en-N030")[#text("30 British cockfights (the sport was banned there in 1840) indeed seem to have lacked it, and to have generated, therefore, a quite different family of shapes. Most British fights were “mains,” in which a preagreed number of cocks were aligned into two teams and fought serially. Score was kept and wagering took place both on the individual matches and on the main as a whole. There were also “battle Royales,” both in England and on the Continent, in which a large number of cocks were let loose at once with the one left standing at the end the victor. And in Wales, the so-called “Welsh main” followed an elimination pattern, along the lines of a present-day tennis tournament, winners proceeding to the next round. As a genre, the cockfight has perhaps less compositional flexibility than, say, Latin comedy, but it is not entirely without any. On cockfighting more generally, see Arch Ruport, The Art of Cockfighting (New York: Devin-Adair, 1949); G. R. Scott, History of Cockfighting (London: Charles Skilton, 1957); and Lawrence Fitz-Barnard, Fighting Sports (London: Odhams Press, 1921).")]

#zh(id: "N030")[#text("30 英国斗鸡运动（这项运动于 1840 年在当地被禁止）似乎确实缺乏这种斗鸡运动，因此产生了一个完全不同的斗鸡运动系列。大多数英国比赛都是“主赛”，即预先约定数量的雄鸽分成两队，连续进行比赛。单场比赛和整场比赛均保留比分并进行投注。在英格兰和欧洲大陆也有“大逃杀”活动，大量雄鸽同时被释放，最后剩下的一只获胜。在威尔士，所谓的“威尔士主赛”遵循淘汰赛模式，类似于当今的网球锦标赛，获胜者进入下一轮。作为一种流派，斗鸡的构图灵活性可能不如拉丁喜剧，但也并非完全没有。关于更一般的斗鸡，请参阅 Arch Ruport，《斗鸡的艺术》（纽约：Devin-Adair，1949 年）； G. R. Scott，《斗鸡史》（伦敦：Charles Skilton，1957 年）；和劳伦斯·菲茨·巴纳德 (Lawrence Fitz-Barnard)，《格斗运动》（伦敦：Odhams Press，1921 年）。")]

#html.p(id: "en-N031")[#text("31 Person, Time, and Conduct, esp. 42ff. I am, however, not the first person to have argued it: see G. Bateson, “Bali, the Value System of a Steady State,” and “An Old Temple and a New Myth,” in Belo, ed., Traditional Balinese Culture, 384–402, 111–136.")]

#zh(id: "N031")[#text("31 人、时间和行为，尤其是。 42页。然而，我并不是第一个提出这一观点的人：参见 G. Bateson，“巴厘岛，稳定国家的价值体系”和“一座古老的寺庙和一个新的神话”，载于贝洛编辑，传统巴厘岛文化，384-402、111-136。")]

